WEBVTT


00:01:58.913 --> 00:02:01.939
It was May 2nd, 1934.


00:02:02.116 --> 00:02:05.813
Even though the Great Depression
was in full swing around the world...


00:02:05.987 --> 00:02:08.888
...it still seemed far
away from the world I knew.


00:02:09.357 --> 00:02:12.520
I was focused on one thing.


00:02:12.694 --> 00:02:15.356
I wanted to be a reporter.


00:02:36.384 --> 00:02:41.754
I was more excited that day than
I'd ever been in my whole life.


00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:48.687
Next up, Clifton and Ludlow.
Everybody up for Clifton and Ludlow.


00:02:50.331 --> 00:02:53.698
If I hurried, I'd have just enough
time to see Dad at his car dealership.


00:02:55.236 --> 00:02:58.399
Dad. Dad. Dad, look,
I finished my article.


00:02:58.573 --> 00:03:00.564
That's great, honey. Oh!


00:03:02.477 --> 00:03:04.672
- Wish me luck.
- You can do it, sweetheart.


00:03:04.846 --> 00:03:06.370
Go get them.


00:03:08.516 --> 00:03:10.507
She's gonna be a reporter, that one.


00:03:10.685 --> 00:03:11.879
You just wait and see.


00:03:13.421 --> 00:03:16.083
Hobo crime spree spreading
across Ohio Valley.


00:03:16.257 --> 00:03:19.454
Come get your paper. Hobo crime spree.


00:03:19.627 --> 00:03:21.185
Hobo crime spree spreading...


00:03:21.362 --> 00:03:24.559
My brother Charlie had
arranged an introduction...


00:03:24.899 --> 00:03:27.891
...at the Cincinnati Register.


00:03:31.205 --> 00:03:33.901
Though I was going
inside as a visitor...


00:03:34.342 --> 00:03:38.108
...I was certain I'd
come out a journalist.


00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:45.610
How could I have known
what life had in store?


00:03:45.787 --> 00:03:48.813
I thought I was ready.


00:03:49.324 --> 00:03:51.053
Seven, please?


00:03:56.531 --> 00:04:00.126
Excuse me, Mr. Peabody? Sir?


00:04:00.301 --> 00:04:02.633
- Charlie Kittredge's sister.
- Yes, sir, I'm Kit.


00:04:02.804 --> 00:04:06.535
Uh, Billy, and I'm only 19. I'm not
a sir yet. But thanks. Uh, follow me.


00:04:06.941 --> 00:04:10.206
Charlie said you wanted to see
a real newsroom, and this is it.


00:04:10.378 --> 00:04:12.471
Wow, the Cincinnati Register.


00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:15.946
- Okay. So where's the editor?
- Uh, Gibson? Right there, why?


00:04:16.417 --> 00:04:19.477
I've written this article, and
I'd like for you to give it to him.


00:04:20.188 --> 00:04:23.555
- Uh, you'd like me to what?
- Give it to him, you know, to publish.


00:04:23.725 --> 00:04:25.158
You're kidding.


00:04:25.693 --> 00:04:27.854
You're not kidding. She's not kidding.


00:04:28.029 --> 00:04:31.123
I'm not kidding. It's about
the Chicago World's Fair.


00:04:31.299 --> 00:04:34.200
I've interviewed several people
who've actually been there.


00:04:34.369 --> 00:04:35.495
Eyewitnesses.


00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.298
Heh. That's what this is about?
You want an article published?


00:04:38.473 --> 00:04:39.497
Yes, thank you.


00:04:39.674 --> 00:04:42.541
Kid, you don't waltz up to the
editor of the Cincinnati Register...


00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.543
...and expect him to
publish your article.


00:04:45.713 --> 00:04:51.549
The tragedy is the fact that I paid
you at all to write this drivel!


00:04:51.719 --> 00:04:54.847
I want something new.
I want something fresh.


00:04:55.023 --> 00:04:57.787
I want something real before I...


00:04:57.959 --> 00:04:59.688
But how about I take a look at it?


00:04:59.861 --> 00:05:02.056
Thanks, Billy, would you?


00:05:03.264 --> 00:05:05.027
I'll just wait here.


00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:07.360
- Ha, ha.
- But you take your time.


00:05:07.535 --> 00:05:08.900
Uh, no.


00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:11.664
You can come back another time.


00:05:12.607 --> 00:05:13.835
That works too.


00:05:14.008 --> 00:05:15.498
He's just reading it, Ruthie.


00:05:15.677 --> 00:05:18.009
He might not even give it
to Mr. Gibson. Who knows?


00:05:18.179 --> 00:05:21.342
- You're late. Let's go.
- Does Mother know you're coming with me?


00:05:21.516 --> 00:05:24.917
Mother, I'm going to
Kit's house with Frances!


00:05:25.086 --> 00:05:27.577
- Yes, dear.
- Now she does.


00:05:33.061 --> 00:05:34.585
Good day.


00:05:35.663 --> 00:05:37.130
Come on, let's go.


00:05:37.298 --> 00:05:39.493
I'm being sworn Into the Treehouse Club


00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:42.602
- I'm being sworn into the Treehouse Club.
- Afternoon.


00:05:44.038 --> 00:05:45.596
Hello.


00:05:45.807 --> 00:05:49.800
You all know if the people who live
here might be interested in a barter?


00:05:50.044 --> 00:05:52.444
Depends. What's a barter?


00:05:52.613 --> 00:05:53.637
A trade.


00:05:54.115 --> 00:05:57.016
See, my partner here and
I'll work for food and...


00:05:57.185 --> 00:06:00.450
- Anyways, I'm Will, and this is Countee.
- Hey.


00:06:01.055 --> 00:06:03.990
Mother's out back with
her Garden Club. Follow me.


00:06:04.158 --> 00:06:06.626
I'm being sworn Into the Treehouse Club.


00:06:06.794 --> 00:06:10.594
Everything will be okay.
Everything will be fine.


00:06:17.038 --> 00:06:19.063
I just heard you're moving, Louise.


00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.537
My husband left weeks ago...


00:06:20.708 --> 00:06:23.609
...and we're staying at the
Netherland until he sends for us.


00:06:23.778 --> 00:06:25.837
The Netherland Plaza Hotel?


00:06:26.013 --> 00:06:27.571
I heard it's magnificent.


00:06:27.749 --> 00:06:29.341
We'd love to see the rooms.


00:06:29.884 --> 00:06:31.579
Margaret, look.


00:06:31.753 --> 00:06:32.947
Mother.


00:06:33.121 --> 00:06:35.749
WO Hobos. We cannot trust them.


00:06:38.826 --> 00:06:40.316
Hello.


00:06:40.495 --> 00:06:41.792
How can I help you?


00:06:41.963 --> 00:06:46.161
- Mother, this is Will
and his friend... Countee.


00:06:46.334 --> 00:06:49.792
And they're looking to make a
barter, which means work for food.


00:06:49.971 --> 00:06:51.996
I can pretty much fix anything broke.


00:06:52.673 --> 00:06:54.197
Windows, uh...


00:06:54.375 --> 00:06:57.640
...fences, any kind of machine.


00:06:57.812 --> 00:07:00.542
We're trustworthy and responsible.


00:07:00.715 --> 00:07:02.376
Come back in the morning.


00:07:02.817 --> 00:07:05.047
We'll see what we can do. Thank you.


00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.212
One moment.


00:07:09.557 --> 00:07:11.115
WO Don't feed them, Margaret.


00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:15.154
Why don't you take something
to eat before you leave?


00:07:15.329 --> 00:07:17.559
Thank you, ma'am, but, uh,
we only take when we work.


00:07:17.932 --> 00:07:19.490
I can't let you leave hungry.


00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:23.467
Better be quick. I've already seen Mrs.
Culver dive headfirst into the nuts.


00:07:24.005 --> 00:07:26.496
Who knows what she'll do
when she sees the sandwiches?


00:07:26.874 --> 00:07:28.501
Please, take it as an advance.


00:07:29.377 --> 00:07:30.503
Go on.


00:07:30.678 --> 00:07:33.146
All right, as an advance. Thank you.


00:07:33.314 --> 00:07:36.511
- We'll be working it off in the morning.
- See you then.


00:07:36.984 --> 00:07:39.145
Let's go, Countee.
- Ahem.


00:07:40.621 --> 00:07:44.284
My husband says let them go hungry. It's
the only way to keep them out of town.


00:07:44.458 --> 00:07:45.982
Oh, Louise.


00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.286
Can't help but think that those
boys are someone's children too.


00:07:49.463 --> 00:07:50.725
Hmm.


00:07:51.365 --> 00:07:53.333
Will, come on.


00:07:55.002 --> 00:07:57.766
Okay, everybody. In your places.


00:07:59.307 --> 00:08:01.002
"Member-o-belia.


00:08:02.343 --> 00:08:05.244
Eleanor Roosevelt. Amelia Earhart.


00:08:05.713 --> 00:08:07.772
Kit, Ruthie, Frances...


00:08:07.949 --> 00:08:10.577
...and soon, Florence.


00:08:10.751 --> 00:08:12.582
Do you, Florence Stone...


00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.786
...swear to be true to the treehouse
Club and only the treehouse Club...


00:08:17.225 --> 00:08:19.352
...as long as you shall live?


00:08:19.527 --> 00:08:20.892
I do.


00:08:21.062 --> 00:08:25.431
And if anyone should ask you
to join the 9th Street Club?


00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.630
I will laugh in their faces.


00:08:28.803 --> 00:08:31.795
You're supposed to
say, "I will decline. "


00:08:32.206 --> 00:08:34.197
I will decline...


00:08:34.375 --> 00:08:36.275
...and laugh.
- Ha, ha.


00:08:37.945 --> 00:08:42.041
Now Ruthie will get the sacred
water off the sacred shelf.


00:08:42.216 --> 00:08:44.411
I already did. It's in the sacred bowl.


00:08:44.852 --> 00:08:46.183
Good.


00:08:46.354 --> 00:08:50.484
Now treehouse members and
potential treehouse members...


00:08:50.658 --> 00:08:54.424
...take your right hand,
and place it in the bowl.


00:08:57.665 --> 00:08:59.132
We are one.


00:09:01.202 --> 00:09:04.103
Now place two fingers
over your heart, like so...


00:09:04.805 --> 00:09:08.002
...for the sacred treehouse pledge.


00:09:08.276 --> 00:09:10.107
All together:


00:09:10.278 --> 00:09:13.076
Gwanga, gwanga, galoolie...


00:09:13.247 --> 00:09:16.375
...kariba, kariba, kariv.


00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.984
- I swear to be true
to the... Betty Lou!


00:09:19.153 --> 00:09:20.848
Robert, do something!


00:09:22.456 --> 00:09:24.549
Sir...? Yep.


00:09:24.959 --> 00:09:27.689
We're gonna be okay then. No, save that.


00:09:27.995 --> 00:09:31.055
Frances, look. That's your bed.


00:09:31.232 --> 00:09:33.063
What does that mean? My furniture.


00:09:33.234 --> 00:09:36.829
"Foreclosure by order of the bank. "


00:09:37.004 --> 00:09:39.097
That means the bank's
taking our house away.


00:09:40.441 --> 00:09:42.875
Ruthie, isn't that your father's bank?


00:09:43.277 --> 00:09:45.472
Can you ask your father to give it back?


00:09:46.847 --> 00:09:51.250
Just let them go. Betty
Lou! Just let them go.


00:09:54.588 --> 00:09:57.921
Oh, what a pity. I had no idea.


00:09:58.092 --> 00:10:00.492
Well, he lost his job
months ago, Lorraine.


00:10:00.661 --> 00:10:03.323
They've been selling
eggs just to get by.


00:10:05.032 --> 00:10:08.490
- Will you excuse me? You
just let them go, okay?


00:10:08.836 --> 00:10:10.133
Come on, let's go.


00:10:10.304 --> 00:10:14.001
But my picture's not up on
the member-o-belia wall yet.


00:10:14.175 --> 00:10:15.904
Florence.


00:10:22.483 --> 00:10:25.316
Everybody's watching, Lou.


00:10:31.359 --> 00:10:32.758
Betty Lou.


00:10:34.662 --> 00:10:36.254
Oh, I'm so sorry.


00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:38.398
Come on.
- Where will they go?


00:10:38.566 --> 00:10:40.500
I don't know.


00:10:41.836 --> 00:10:44.327
I'm so sorry, Barbara.


00:10:59.353 --> 00:11:01.787
There she is. How's my girl?


00:11:01.956 --> 00:11:03.583
- Dad, Dad.
- Come here.


00:11:06.994 --> 00:11:10.020
- What's the matter?
- Dad, look.


00:11:11.932 --> 00:11:15.265
"The Stones lose their house. "


00:11:20.808 --> 00:11:23.276
And that's why my favorite
stories are fairy tales...


00:11:23.611 --> 00:11:26.409
...because no matter
how scary it gets...


00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.707
...everybody always
lives happily ever after.


00:11:30.217 --> 00:11:32.549
And often with some very nice outfits.


00:11:35.823 --> 00:11:39.657
Thank you, Ruthie. Thank
you. Thank you, Ruthie.


00:11:41.195 --> 00:11:44.130
Does, uh... Does anyone
know where Frances Stone is?


00:11:44.298 --> 00:11:45.492
She's away.


00:11:45.666 --> 00:11:48.134
Staying with family.


00:11:48.302 --> 00:11:51.635
- Yeah, in a hobo jungle somewhere.
- Stop it, Roger.


00:11:51.806 --> 00:11:54.570
Frances Stone is a good-for-nothing,
deadbeat egg seller...


00:11:54.742 --> 00:11:57.711
...and everyone knows it. She
wore dresses made of feed sacks.


00:11:57.878 --> 00:12:01.109
Roger. Ruthie, not another word.


00:12:01.816 --> 00:12:04.580
Well, this seems like an
opportune time to let you know...


00:12:04.752 --> 00:12:07.585
...that as a final class
assignment before summer vacation...


00:12:07.755 --> 00:12:10.087
...we're going to help
out at a soup kitchen...


00:12:10.257 --> 00:12:12.589
...where they provide food
for the poor and needy.


00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.318
Uh, Roger.


00:12:14.495 --> 00:12:16.588
My dad says that people
eat at soup kitchens...


00:12:16.764 --> 00:12:20.200
...are hobos who feed off the government.
That's why we're in a depression.


00:12:20.601 --> 00:12:22.535
I understand, Roger.


00:12:22.703 --> 00:12:26.070
Therefore, anyone who
doesn't wish to help out...


00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:29.767
...can write a 1500-word essay
on the history of volunteerism.


00:12:31.912 --> 00:12:34.472
Roger's leading my team
for the soup kitchen.


00:12:34.648 --> 00:12:35.842
Why, he's a nice boy.


00:12:36.016 --> 00:12:40.544
He's a horrible boy. He just didn't
want to have to do that paper.


00:12:44.358 --> 00:12:47.987
Mother, look. She's all alone.


00:12:48.162 --> 00:12:50.392
Oh. Poor thing.


00:12:53.033 --> 00:12:56.059
"Grace. Can't feed her anymore. "


00:12:58.038 --> 00:12:59.403
Can we take her?


00:13:00.074 --> 00:13:01.132
Wha...? No.


00:13:01.308 --> 00:13:05.836
She can be my birthday present
for this year and next year...


00:13:06.013 --> 00:13:07.139
...and forever.


00:13:07.314 --> 00:13:09.077
I'm sorry, sweetheart.


00:13:12.386 --> 00:13:13.717
Wha...? What's that, Grace?


00:13:13.888 --> 00:13:16.152
- Ha-ha-ha.
Mm-hm. Mm-hm.


00:13:17.558 --> 00:13:19.389
Okay, I'll tell her.


00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.654
She said she'll be very
quiet and only eat scraps...


00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.491
- Mm-hm. - And be
the very best dog...


00:13:24.665 --> 00:13:26.030
...you could ever hope to have.


00:13:27.868 --> 00:13:34.171
Can we at least bring her home for
one teeny-weeny meaningless night?


00:13:34.341 --> 00:13:36.605
How can you say no to that face?


00:13:37.144 --> 00:13:39.009
Mother.


00:13:39.847 --> 00:13:41.212
Please, please, please.


00:13:41.849 --> 00:13:43.510
All right, but one night only.


00:13:44.318 --> 00:13:47.515
She said yes. She said
yes. You're coming home.


00:13:49.823 --> 00:13:52.018
I'll take care of you. Don't worry.


00:13:54.795 --> 00:13:57.093
Help! Stop him!


00:13:57.264 --> 00:13:59.698
Help, stop, stop him. Help!


00:13:59.867 --> 00:14:02.301
Stop him. Police. What happened?


00:14:02.469 --> 00:14:05.404
Did you see that? In broad daylight.


00:14:05.573 --> 00:14:07.768
- He took my wallet.
- Are you all right?


00:14:08.242 --> 00:14:10.642
Yes, ma'am.
- What color was your wallet?


00:14:10.811 --> 00:14:13.871
It was red. My son made it for me.


00:14:14.048 --> 00:14:15.913
Did you see that tattoo on his arm?


00:14:16.083 --> 00:14:18.278
It was odd. Something
like a bird, maybe.


00:14:18.586 --> 00:14:20.918
It was so fast, I didn't
really get to see it all.


00:14:21.088 --> 00:14:25.422
All I noticed was that he was a hobo.


00:14:28.095 --> 00:14:29.562
Get your dog.


00:14:35.736 --> 00:14:37.966
I had a late lunch today, Margaret.


00:14:38.639 --> 00:14:39.833
Save it for leftovers.


00:14:43.444 --> 00:14:46.242
- Uncle Hendrick, more meatloaf?
- Thank you, no.


00:14:46.413 --> 00:14:49.905
I'll tell you this, Jack, keep supporting
your president and his New Deal...


00:14:50.084 --> 00:14:52.609
...and you'll see what'll
happen to this country.


00:14:52.786 --> 00:14:54.048
Your own son quit college.


00:14:54.221 --> 00:14:58.089
Well, he didn't quit.
He postponed. Right, Dad?


00:14:58.259 --> 00:15:01.319
To join one of these
newfangled programs.


00:15:01.495 --> 00:15:06.398
CCC is a great program, Hendrick. Charlie
is building parks and fixing roads...


00:15:06.567 --> 00:15:10.298
And sending money home because
business is a little slow.


00:15:10.838 --> 00:15:13.033
There's no shame in
families helping each other.


00:15:13.207 --> 00:15:14.902
Hmm.


00:15:15.075 --> 00:15:17.441
Hard to think of you this way, Margaret.


00:15:17.611 --> 00:15:19.636
Saving leftovers.


00:15:20.581 --> 00:15:22.446
We like leftovers.


00:15:23.917 --> 00:15:28.377
- I should be leaving.
- Oh, no. Please. Stay longer.


00:15:28.555 --> 00:15:30.921
Ha-ha-ha. You think I'm
the voice of doom, Jack.


00:15:31.091 --> 00:15:33.958
But I'll tell you this. When
people live beyond their means...


00:15:34.128 --> 00:15:36.790
...in houses that are too
big for their pockets...


00:15:38.565 --> 00:15:39.998
What's that noise?


00:15:40.167 --> 00:15:43.193
Oh, you haven't met our
newest addition, Grace.


00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.770
She's normally very
quiet, Uncle Hendrick.


00:15:45.939 --> 00:15:48.908
Except for when she
snores. And the drool.


00:15:49.076 --> 00:15:53.410
It seems to me the last thing
you need is another mouth to feed.


00:15:53.914 --> 00:15:56.747
Well, luckily, she
doesn't eat very much.


00:15:58.085 --> 00:15:59.484
Come on, Grace. Come on.


00:15:59.653 --> 00:16:02.520
Good girl.


00:16:07.127 --> 00:16:10.995
Shouldn't you fellows be getting
on back to wherever it is you live?


00:16:11.799 --> 00:16:13.391
Yes, sir.


00:16:15.402 --> 00:16:17.427
What's the capital of
North Dakota? Quick.


00:16:17.604 --> 00:16:18.662
Bismarck.


00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:21.870
Make sure your mother
doesn't work too hard.


00:16:39.493 --> 00:16:43.520
I can't believe that none of
these people have money for food.


00:16:45.065 --> 00:16:47.727
You come over here and chop
that bread up for everybody...


00:16:47.901 --> 00:16:50.369
...back of the line, right there.


00:17:10.924 --> 00:17:14.655
- Would you like some
bread? Thanks, sweetheart.


00:17:14.828 --> 00:17:16.420
Excuse me.


00:17:44.558 --> 00:17:46.116
Dad.


00:18:27.868 --> 00:18:29.165
Tried.


00:18:34.942 --> 00:18:36.307
How is she?


00:18:36.477 --> 00:18:39.139
Uh, she's... She's up in the tree house.


00:18:47.788 --> 00:18:49.221
Kit.


00:18:52.626 --> 00:18:54.218
Kit, I know you're up there.


00:18:55.229 --> 00:18:58.062
I understand if you're angry with me...


00:18:58.232 --> 00:19:00.200
...or, uh, embarrassed.


00:19:01.001 --> 00:19:04.835
Please come down, sweetheart.
I'd like to explain.


00:19:08.475 --> 00:19:09.840
Kit.


00:19:34.535 --> 00:19:35.729
Kit.


00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:45.300
Why were you there, Dad?


00:19:48.849 --> 00:19:51.079
The bank took the car dealership.


00:19:52.319 --> 00:19:56.187
- Ruthie's father did that to us?
- Kit.


00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.151
This isn't personal.


00:19:59.226 --> 00:20:00.887
Are we gonna lose the house?


00:20:02.829 --> 00:20:04.490
We're gonna be fine.


00:20:10.604 --> 00:20:12.401
Where's our car?


00:20:15.008 --> 00:20:16.532
Daddy.


00:20:20.948 --> 00:20:22.711
We're gonna be okay.


00:20:23.617 --> 00:20:27.610
Hey, Kit, your father good enough
to eat at the soup kitchen? Ha, ha!


00:20:27.788 --> 00:20:28.982
Just ignore them.


00:20:29.156 --> 00:20:32.216
And now Kit will probably start
raising chickens, selling eggs...


00:20:32.392 --> 00:20:37.091
...and wearing feed-sack
dresses, just like Frances did.


00:20:37.264 --> 00:20:38.822
What's a feed-sack dress?


00:20:38.999 --> 00:20:42.025
It's a dress made out of the
bag that chicken feed comes in.


00:20:42.336 --> 00:20:44.201
Are you really gonna wear one, Kit?


00:20:44.371 --> 00:20:47.363
Sure she will. Right after
her family starts selling eggs.


00:20:47.674 --> 00:20:49.266
We're not egg sellers.


00:20:49.443 --> 00:20:51.843
And then it's just one
step before the poorhouse.


00:20:57.351 --> 00:21:00.445
Come get your chicken and your
eggs from Kit Kittredge. Bock, bock!


00:21:00.621 --> 00:21:02.282
You're all a bunch of freeloaders.


00:21:04.391 --> 00:21:07.155
Why don't you come over here
and say that to my face, Roger.


00:21:07.327 --> 00:21:10.888
Okay. You're all a bunch of freeloaders!


00:21:11.598 --> 00:21:13.293
What's that you've got behind you?


00:21:17.471 --> 00:21:19.996
Got any eggs? Bock, bock, bock!


00:21:20.173 --> 00:21:22.232
How much for the eggs, Roger?


00:21:22.409 --> 00:21:25.242
Bock, bock, bock! Look
at Mr. Feather Pants.


00:21:25.412 --> 00:21:28.006
Ha-ha-ha! - And
how's our mural...


00:21:29.983 --> 00:21:31.007
...coming?


00:21:31.184 --> 00:21:34.915
But, Mother, it was Roger's fault.
- Oh, Kit.


00:21:35.088 --> 00:21:38.148
Please don't take everything
people say so personally.


00:21:39.059 --> 00:21:41.220
Everyone's in trouble these days.


00:21:41.395 --> 00:21:44.159
In fact, we've had to move
your things into the attic.


00:21:44.531 --> 00:21:47.056
The attic? Why?


00:21:47.234 --> 00:21:50.362
Your mother and I have decided
we're gonna take in boarders.


00:21:51.772 --> 00:21:54.138
Boarders? Like...


00:21:54.307 --> 00:21:55.569
...strangers?


00:21:55.742 --> 00:22:01.339
That are gonna come and live in our house
and eat our food and sit on our sofa?


00:22:01.682 --> 00:22:04.344
There's no shame in it, honey.


00:22:04.518 --> 00:22:06.713
Lots of people are doing it to get by.


00:22:06.887 --> 00:22:09.219
We're not gonna start
selling eggs, are we?


00:22:09.389 --> 00:22:11.357
Ha, ha. We hadn't planned on it.


00:22:12.225 --> 00:22:15.388
This is only temporary,
sweetheart, I promise.


00:22:15.562 --> 00:22:16.927
You know...


00:22:17.097 --> 00:22:18.394
...once I get to Chicago...


00:22:18.799 --> 00:22:22.667
...I'll find a good-paying job, just
as quick as you can say "Plymouth. "


00:22:22.836 --> 00:22:24.064
Chicago?


00:22:24.371 --> 00:22:26.100
What? You're leaving?


00:22:26.273 --> 00:22:27.763
- I have some good leads in...
- No.


00:22:28.341 --> 00:22:29.501
- No.
- Kit.


00:22:29.676 --> 00:22:32.167
You said we were going to be okay.


00:22:32.345 --> 00:22:34.905
We're not okay if we're not together.


00:22:48.895 --> 00:22:50.123
Ah.


00:22:50.297 --> 00:22:51.662
Don't let it beat you, Kit.


00:22:54.367 --> 00:22:55.925
You know, when I was about 14...


00:22:56.336 --> 00:23:00.568
...my dad traded some old farm
equipment for a used Model t.


00:23:00.741 --> 00:23:03.335
One time I was cranking
it, to start it...


00:23:04.277 --> 00:23:06.711
...and the crank broke in half.


00:23:07.114 --> 00:23:10.311
Car kind of groaned, and it just
died right in our front yard.


00:23:11.384 --> 00:23:13.978
I wanted to junk the darn
thing when my dad walked in...


00:23:14.821 --> 00:23:17.415
...and he said, "Don't
let it beat you, son. "


00:23:18.024 --> 00:23:19.958
Don't let it beat you.


00:23:20.627 --> 00:23:23.460
Drove it for five more years after that.


00:23:24.431 --> 00:23:27.491
So whether it's an old Model t...


00:23:27.801 --> 00:23:30.167
...or a typewriter ribbon...


00:23:31.471 --> 00:23:35.339
...or going to Chicago to find work...


00:23:39.646 --> 00:23:41.614
...we can't let it beat us, sweetheart.


00:23:42.783 --> 00:23:43.807
Come on.


00:23:44.451 --> 00:23:46.180
It's time for bed.


00:23:54.427 --> 00:23:56.657
What if you don't come back?


00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:09.772
I, Jack Kittredge...


00:24:09.943 --> 00:24:11.001
...do solemnly swear...


00:24:11.611 --> 00:24:14.478
...that I will write you once a week.


00:24:14.848 --> 00:24:16.975
And that there is
nothing in this world...


00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:19.482
...that could ever keep
me from coming back to you.


00:24:21.021 --> 00:24:23.353
Do you promise, Dad?


00:24:23.523 --> 00:24:25.218
Really promise?


00:24:25.759 --> 00:24:27.693
I do.


00:24:28.895 --> 00:24:31.227
Gwanga, gwanga, galoolie...


00:24:33.767 --> 00:24:36.827
Kariba, kariba, kariv.


00:24:42.175 --> 00:24:44.439
I love you, Dad.


00:24:45.445 --> 00:24:49.006
- I love you.
- I love you too, sweetheart.


00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:05.793
Bye.


00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:27.076
June 17th, 1934. Dear Dad.


00:25:28.021 --> 00:25:32.549
Thanks for the letter. We miss you too
and hope you're finding work in Chicago.


00:25:32.726 --> 00:25:34.353
Here at home, things are hopping.


00:25:34.527 --> 00:25:38.224
School's out, and I can't call my friends
because the phone's been disconnected.


00:25:38.398 --> 00:25:39.422
No phone?


00:25:39.599 --> 00:25:43.626
So now all I do is clean
and sweep and fold...


00:25:43.803 --> 00:25:46.465
...and iron and wipe and cook.


00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:49.803
And fold and clean and sweep and
iron and wipe and cook some more.


00:25:49.976 --> 00:25:54.037
My grand plans for being a real
reporter are kind of on the back burner.


00:25:54.214 --> 00:25:57.581
Don't get me wrong, Dad. I'm
not complaining. I'm just...


00:25:57.751 --> 00:26:00.743
...well, complaining.


00:26:02.522 --> 00:26:05.582
The house is chock-full of
boarders, which keeps us pretty busy.


00:26:06.126 --> 00:26:09.789
Mrs. Howard and her son,
Stirling, moved into my bedroom.


00:26:12.365 --> 00:26:13.696
We're almost there, lamby.


00:26:15.001 --> 00:26:17.094
Lamby.


00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:20.769
It's going to be strange having
a boy from my class living here.


00:26:20.941 --> 00:26:22.704
But I guess I'll get used to it.


00:26:23.176 --> 00:26:26.475
Mother moved into the sewing room
so we can squeeze in another border.


00:26:26.646 --> 00:26:28.511
I thought that was very clever of her.


00:26:29.049 --> 00:26:31.074
Now your room belongs to Miss Dooley...


00:26:31.251 --> 00:26:35.051
...a dance instructor who likes
to practice her steps out back.


00:26:35.221 --> 00:26:38.315
Though, I'm not at all
sure Mrs. Howard approves.


00:26:39.526 --> 00:26:42.017
Did you know Mother divided
the living room in half...


00:26:42.195 --> 00:26:45.096
...to make room for Miss Bond, a
mobile librarian who moved in last week?


00:26:46.466 --> 00:26:49.401
Now, if she can only learn the difference
between the gas and the brake...


00:26:51.204 --> 00:26:53.195
Oh, take cover!


00:26:54.007 --> 00:26:55.634
We'll all breathe a little easier.


00:27:03.049 --> 00:27:04.812
Where'd everyone go?


00:27:05.018 --> 00:27:06.986
You'll be happy to
know we've been using...


00:27:07.153 --> 00:27:10.145
...Aunt Millie's Waste Not, Want
Not tips to make meals stretch.


00:27:10.323 --> 00:27:12.848
Like cutting one piece of
toast into four triangles...


00:27:13.026 --> 00:27:15.358
...to make a whole
loaf go for three meals.


00:27:16.262 --> 00:27:17.752
Mother says it's magic.


00:27:17.931 --> 00:27:21.423
- But the real magician is our newest arrival...
- Jefferson J. Berk...


00:27:21.668 --> 00:27:27.231
...master of misdirection, dean of
deception, and escape artist extraordinaire.


00:27:27.407 --> 00:27:30.501
Observe. I need a
volunteer from the audience.


00:27:31.578 --> 00:27:33.102
Young man.


00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:35.645
- What's your name?
- Stirling.


00:27:35.815 --> 00:27:37.043
Stirling.


00:27:37.217 --> 00:27:39.447
- Do you mind wearing
these for a while? No. No.


00:27:39.619 --> 00:27:40.847
Come with me.


00:27:41.021 --> 00:27:47.017
Notice the strong solid-steel
cuffs. Can you get out of that?


00:27:47.193 --> 00:27:49.093
- Are you sure? Are you sure?
- Yeah.


00:27:49.262 --> 00:27:50.524
Are you sure? Ha, ha!


00:27:50.697 --> 00:27:53.894
The only thing that can get
you out of that is this key.


00:27:54.401 --> 00:27:56.392
We hope. Sometimes it
doesn't work though.


00:27:56.569 --> 00:27:59.094
And then we have to cut you out of it.


00:27:59.406 --> 00:28:03.365
Oh, there we are. All right, now,
would you hold this key for me, please?


00:28:03.543 --> 00:28:04.601
Yes.


00:28:05.111 --> 00:28:08.046
Don't lose it. Will you put
these on my wrists, please?


00:28:08.214 --> 00:28:09.613
Yes.


00:28:11.918 --> 00:28:13.146
Now.


00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:16.387
Would you like me to
free myself from these?


00:28:16.556 --> 00:28:19.787
With brute force or with magic?


00:28:19.959 --> 00:28:21.984
Magic. Magic.


00:28:22.162 --> 00:28:24.596
All right, then. All right, then.


00:28:25.298 --> 00:28:27.357
Let's count. See how long it takes.


00:28:29.502 --> 00:28:33.734
One, two, three...


00:28:36.009 --> 00:28:37.271
Wow.


00:28:39.479 --> 00:28:41.140
Wow!


00:28:41.681 --> 00:28:44.650
- How'd you do that?
- It's magic.


00:28:45.718 --> 00:28:49.654
And, Dad, please remember, I
miss you more than you know.


00:28:49.823 --> 00:28:53.589
We all do. Never forget that.


00:28:55.962 --> 00:28:57.589
Hi.


00:28:58.898 --> 00:28:59.922
Hi.


00:29:03.036 --> 00:29:04.367
Sorry.


00:29:04.804 --> 00:29:06.294
Allergies.


00:29:06.706 --> 00:29:08.469
These are from your room.


00:29:09.042 --> 00:29:11.374
Uh, just leave them there.


00:29:11.544 --> 00:29:13.409
I'll put them away.


00:29:20.386 --> 00:29:22.217
Is that...


00:29:22.989 --> 00:29:24.684
...Ernie Lombardi?


00:29:24.858 --> 00:29:26.257
Yep.


00:29:27.827 --> 00:29:29.692
A picture of the Schnoz.


00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:33.695
Did you know that he's the
biggest player on the team?


00:29:33.867 --> 00:29:36.700
Six-foot-three,
230 pounds.


00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:38.132
He's my favorite.


00:29:38.304 --> 00:29:41.398
That's funny. He's got a
huge nose and you've got...


00:29:41.574 --> 00:29:43.064
Huge ears.


00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:45.468
- I know.
- Ha, ha.


00:29:45.645 --> 00:29:47.579
That's why I like him.


00:29:49.149 --> 00:29:51.982
I'm sorry we put you out of your room.


00:29:52.285 --> 00:29:55.083
That's all right. I can
get more writing done here.


00:29:55.822 --> 00:29:58.382
- What do you write?
- News articles.


00:30:00.059 --> 00:30:02.755
- For your family?
- And for the Cincinnati Register.


00:30:03.229 --> 00:30:05.060
You write for the newspaper?


00:30:06.766 --> 00:30:10.600
Well, I haven't been in "print"
print yet. But I hope to be.


00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:14.001
My brother's friend is a big
reporter for the Register.


00:30:14.174 --> 00:30:16.108
Sorry, kiddo, not for the Register.


00:30:16.276 --> 00:30:19.040
But everyone wants to know
about the Chicago World's Fair.


00:30:19.212 --> 00:30:23.546
Everyone and their second cousin's
written about the Chicago World's Fair.


00:30:25.251 --> 00:30:28.049
- You know what Mr. Gibson says?
- Yeah, yeah, do it.


00:30:36.763 --> 00:30:39.027
I want something new.
I want something fresh.


00:30:39.199 --> 00:30:42.032
I want something real. Don't
sugarcoat your stories, boys.


00:30:42.202 --> 00:30:45.729
This is the Cincinnati Register,
a beacon of cold, clear light...


00:30:45.905 --> 00:30:48.999
...in a sea of sentimental flop.


00:30:49.943 --> 00:30:52.639
So, what were you saying, Mr?


00:30:52.812 --> 00:30:56.908
Uh, Peabody. Sir, uh,
William Peabody and nothing.


00:30:57.116 --> 00:30:59.550
- I was just quoting you, sir.
- Oh.


00:31:00.153 --> 00:31:02.383
Wait, Mr. Gibson.


00:31:02.655 --> 00:31:03.952
Well.


00:31:04.991 --> 00:31:07.152
Hello, sweetie, what's your name?


00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:09.885
Kit Kittredge. Well, actually,
it's Margaret Mildred...


00:31:10.063 --> 00:31:14.090
...after my mother and an aunt. But when
I was younger, my father used to sing:


00:31:14.267 --> 00:31:18.169
Pack up your troubles
in your old kit bag


00:31:18.705 --> 00:31:19.933
And it sort of stuck.


00:31:21.140 --> 00:31:22.266
Sir.


00:31:22.442 --> 00:31:24.467
- Fascinating.
- Wait.


00:31:26.579 --> 00:31:29.241
I wrote an article I
think you should publish.


00:31:29.415 --> 00:31:30.404
Oh.


00:31:30.583 --> 00:31:33.416
Well, thank you very much.


00:31:34.153 --> 00:31:37.179
And, by the way, I don't lisp.


00:32:05.852 --> 00:32:08.514
Whoopsy daisy.


00:32:10.623 --> 00:32:13.148
Whoo! Are you all right?


00:32:13.326 --> 00:32:15.521
Uh, yeah, as long as that horn works.


00:32:15.695 --> 00:32:18.220
Ha, ha. Now, can I
interest you in a book?


00:32:20.533 --> 00:32:24.128
- I don't have much time for reading.
- Well, you should take the time to read.


00:32:24.304 --> 00:32:28.240
It's a wonderful way
to go wherever you want.


00:32:28.408 --> 00:32:32.242
I've just finished reading this one.


00:32:33.346 --> 00:32:34.711
The Adventures of Robin Hood?


00:32:34.881 --> 00:32:37.782
He steals from the rich, and
then he gives to the poor.


00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:40.851
And then what happened to him?
- Well, he'll have to tell you.


00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:43.147
What about you, Countee?
Would you like one?


00:32:43.423 --> 00:32:44.981
I can only read Hobo.


00:32:45.792 --> 00:32:48.056
Oh, I've never tried that. Hmm.


00:32:48.227 --> 00:32:51.355
How about I teach you to read
books, and you teach me to read Hobo?


00:32:51.531 --> 00:32:52.998
That'd be swell, Stirling.


00:32:54.834 --> 00:32:55.994
How about this one?


00:32:56.569 --> 00:32:58.196
Thank you.


00:33:01.107 --> 00:33:04.133
- It's a loaner, though.
- Okay.


00:33:08.981 --> 00:33:10.073
Will?


00:33:12.685 --> 00:33:14.414
- Are you okay?
- Ha, ha.


00:33:15.288 --> 00:33:16.721
Will?


00:33:19.058 --> 00:33:21.891
I want you to have Mr.
Kittredge's old boots.


00:33:22.762 --> 00:33:24.024
No, ma'am, I couldn't.


00:33:24.197 --> 00:33:27.257
You've done wonderful work. Beyond
what you and Countee have eaten.


00:33:27.433 --> 00:33:30.925
I insist you take these before
those things fall off your feet.


00:33:33.339 --> 00:33:34.806
Thank you.


00:33:35.141 --> 00:33:36.540
Go on.


00:33:39.045 --> 00:33:41.513
- Miss Bond.
- Mrs. Kittredge.


00:33:41.681 --> 00:33:43.273
Will.


00:33:44.517 --> 00:33:46.280
Ruthie.


00:33:48.454 --> 00:33:50.649
Hi, Ruthie. What's wrong?


00:33:50.823 --> 00:33:53.724
- Morning.
- We're going on vacation.


00:33:53.893 --> 00:33:57.454
Myrtle Beach, 10 days, starting Friday.


00:33:57.630 --> 00:34:01.293
- And that's bad because...?
It's so much more fun here.


00:34:01.467 --> 00:34:04.402
You're all I ever talk about
at home. Even ask my mom.


00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:06.765
The other day, I was telling
her about you, Miss Dooley.


00:34:06.939 --> 00:34:08.566
Aw, thank you.


00:34:08.741 --> 00:34:11.301
She didn't say what she
was saying about you.


00:34:13.579 --> 00:34:17.345
You know what you need? A novelette.


00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:19.449
What are you writing?


00:34:19.619 --> 00:34:22.144
You just gave me a great
idea for an article.


00:34:22.321 --> 00:34:24.915
I wanna write it down before I forget.


00:34:25.224 --> 00:34:26.589
Here.


00:34:27.326 --> 00:34:30.489
I'll do this for you, and you go
type it up before you forget, okay?


00:34:31.364 --> 00:34:33.628
- Are you sure?
- Go.


00:34:34.267 --> 00:34:36.667
Thanks, Ruthie.
- I have just the thing.


00:34:39.839 --> 00:34:41.807
They fit real good.


00:34:42.742 --> 00:34:44.300
Thank you.


00:34:47.146 --> 00:34:48.773
Pretty.


00:34:54.987 --> 00:34:56.887
Wish me luck.


00:34:59.792 --> 00:35:01.225
Who is it?


00:35:01.394 --> 00:35:03.191
Margaret Mildred Kittredge.


00:35:04.597 --> 00:35:07.122
- What do you want?
- To be in print.


00:35:07.600 --> 00:35:10.330
Well, how do I say this nicely?


00:35:15.374 --> 00:35:18.605
- But you haven't even read it!
- She's sorry, Mr. Gibson.


00:35:18.778 --> 00:35:21.076
- Come on, Kit.
- Yeah, that's right, I am sorry.


00:35:21.247 --> 00:35:26.310
I'm sorry you can't recognize a good story
when it's standing right outside your door.


00:35:26.786 --> 00:35:28.048
What did you say?


00:35:29.989 --> 00:35:34.119
Portrait of a boarding house, sir.
It's fresh. It's new. It's real.


00:35:34.293 --> 00:35:38.696
What you're holding in your hand, sir,
is the story you've been looking for.


00:35:38.865 --> 00:35:42.733
And you can throw me out if you want,
but I suggest that you read it first.


00:35:43.135 --> 00:35:45.603
"A Kid's-Eye View of the
Depression in Cincinnati. "


00:35:45.771 --> 00:35:47.898
By Kit Kittredge, age 10.


00:35:48.074 --> 00:35:50.474
Ha, ha. That's enough.


00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:15.424
You wrote this?


00:36:16.168 --> 00:36:17.567
Yes.


00:36:18.905 --> 00:36:19.929
It's not bad.


00:36:21.474 --> 00:36:25.342
Are you going to print it, Mr. Gibson?
- I'm sorry, kid.


00:36:25.845 --> 00:36:28.040
"Not bad" is good...


00:36:28.214 --> 00:36:29.875
...but it's not good enough.


00:36:32.051 --> 00:36:35.987
Keep it up, kid. We pay a
penny a word for freelance.


00:36:36.522 --> 00:36:40.424
One, two, three, four,
five, six, seven, eight.


00:36:41.227 --> 00:36:44.025
And guess what. They pay a penny a word.


00:36:44.196 --> 00:36:47.427
Which means, I can make two or
three dollars from just one article.


00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:49.932
Whoo-ee!


00:36:50.102 --> 00:36:53.970
I was thinking about doing a story on
you and Countee, Will, if you let me.


00:36:54.206 --> 00:36:55.764
What's so interesting about us?


00:36:55.942 --> 00:36:58.775
I'd like to find out,
shadow you for the day.


00:36:58.945 --> 00:37:02.142
- That's reporter talk.
- Oh, yeah.


00:37:02.315 --> 00:37:05.842
Well, actually, I'm on my
way out to get a few things...


00:37:06.018 --> 00:37:07.315
...at my camp.


00:37:07.853 --> 00:37:09.320
You can come if you'd like.


00:37:10.623 --> 00:37:12.955
Unless you need us here, Mother.


00:37:13.192 --> 00:37:14.750
No, I can manage.


00:37:15.394 --> 00:37:17.089
Come on, Countee.


00:37:23.135 --> 00:37:26.161
Ruthie, Stirling. Would you
like to be my assistants?


00:37:26.606 --> 00:37:28.471
- Do we get paid?
- No.


00:37:28.774 --> 00:37:30.867
Okay. Yeah.


00:37:31.477 --> 00:37:35.174
Will, Countee was saying
something about reading Hobo signs?


00:37:35.348 --> 00:37:38.340
You know, he can read a sign on
a gatepost about 200 yards out.


00:37:38.517 --> 00:37:42.647
- He's saved my hide about a hundred times.
- What kind of sign's on a gatepost?


00:37:42.822 --> 00:37:44.949
The ones that hobos
leave to help each other.


00:37:46.158 --> 00:37:48.456
- You see that?
- Looks like a cat.


00:37:48.628 --> 00:37:51.222
It is a cat. It's a good sign too.


00:37:51.664 --> 00:37:53.928
It means a kind lady lives here.


00:37:54.467 --> 00:37:58.130
- Not many houses want hobos, you know.
- You had a cat on your gate, Kit.


00:37:58.871 --> 00:38:03.137
- We had a cat.
- Well, I live over there. Do I have a cat?


00:38:03.309 --> 00:38:06.335
- Oh, I hope we have a cat.
- I'm sure you have a cat.


00:38:09.215 --> 00:38:10.705
Sorry, Ruthie. No cat.


00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:13.711
But you do have fish bones, see?


00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:18.249
- Oh, what's that mean?
- Good garbage.


00:38:23.362 --> 00:38:26.195
So, what's it like
riding the rails, Will?


00:38:26.365 --> 00:38:28.094
Train whistles.


00:38:28.267 --> 00:38:31.703
Engines smokes up, and
you run and you jump.


00:38:32.138 --> 00:38:34.868
And you let it take
you to a whole new life.


00:38:35.508 --> 00:38:38.568
- Makes a man feel free. Right, Countee?
- That's right.


00:38:39.311 --> 00:38:41.176
So where's home, Will?


00:38:41.347 --> 00:38:43.178
Uh...


00:38:43.349 --> 00:38:45.112
texas.


00:38:45.418 --> 00:38:48.478
We had a farm. Crops
failed two years in a row.


00:38:48.654 --> 00:38:50.849
Daddy fought to keep it,
but when he couldn't...


00:38:51.023 --> 00:38:54.550
...they headed out west with Belle
and Evelyn and my younger sisters.


00:38:54.727 --> 00:38:56.490
They wanted me to go too...


00:38:56.662 --> 00:38:59.529
...but I figured it was time
for me to strike out on my own...


00:38:59.699 --> 00:39:04.033
...so I rode the rails, uh, made a
few friends and worked when I could.


00:39:04.537 --> 00:39:09.236
That's when I met Countee and his
father in Oklahoma about 14 months ago.


00:39:09.408 --> 00:39:11.638
Kind of stuck together after that.


00:39:11.811 --> 00:39:14.439
- Called ourselves...
- "the three Musketeers. "


00:39:15.381 --> 00:39:17.975
- Ha, ha.
- Yes, we did.


00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:22.646
And, um...


00:39:25.257 --> 00:39:27.418
And then the influenza came.


00:39:28.327 --> 00:39:30.727
And my daddy didn't make it.


00:39:34.400 --> 00:39:36.265
Now it's just us.


00:39:37.369 --> 00:39:39.064
Thank the good Lord I've got him.


00:39:39.405 --> 00:39:42.932
Soon we're gonna make it out to
California and meet the rest of the family.


00:39:43.109 --> 00:39:44.667
Are they gonna like me?


00:39:47.246 --> 00:39:48.713
No.


00:39:51.217 --> 00:39:54.618
I'm just playing. Come on, let's go.


00:39:55.755 --> 00:39:56.915
Let's get going.


00:40:00.092 --> 00:40:01.320
You know what that means?


00:40:01.927 --> 00:40:03.394
Does it mean we're close?


00:40:03.562 --> 00:40:05.553
That means we're near a hobo jungle.


00:40:05.731 --> 00:40:07.528
What is a hobo, exactly?


00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:10.931
A person who hops the rails, going
from town to town looking for work.


00:40:12.037 --> 00:40:13.527
Like a lot of these folks right here.


00:40:16.575 --> 00:40:19.510
Here it is. Home sweet home.


00:40:20.179 --> 00:40:21.305
Come on.


00:40:25.751 --> 00:40:28.481
Life inside a hobo
jungle sure isn't easy.


00:40:28.654 --> 00:40:31.680
These people once had houses and jobs.


00:40:31.857 --> 00:40:34.417
I guess we're all just a few
strokes of bad luck away...


00:40:34.593 --> 00:40:36.424
...from being in that exact same spot.


00:40:36.595 --> 00:40:37.619
Eddie, down there.


00:40:37.797 --> 00:40:39.628
Every day's a challenge for food.


00:40:39.799 --> 00:40:42.233
In most camps, hobos
share what they have:


00:40:42.401 --> 00:40:46.394
Vegetables, potatoes and,
on a good day, maybe meat.


00:40:46.572 --> 00:40:48.836
Would you mind if I took
a couple pictures of you?


00:40:49.008 --> 00:40:50.339
Of course you can.


00:40:50.509 --> 00:40:54.809
They put it in a pot and cook it
all together and call it hobo stew.


00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:57.312
- Those that can contribute
do. Did you get meat?


00:40:57.483 --> 00:41:00.748
Those that can't try
harder the next day.


00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:03.320
But everyone's welcome to share.


00:41:04.290 --> 00:41:05.848
They even have a hobo store...


00:41:06.025 --> 00:41:09.085
...where you leave what you
don't want and take what you do.


00:41:11.564 --> 00:41:15.330
For the most part, they seem like
a trustworthy group of people.


00:41:15.501 --> 00:41:18.368
But trouble must follow them a lot.


00:41:18.537 --> 00:41:21.734
The first sign a hobo learns is danger.


00:41:24.977 --> 00:41:27.104
They needed to protect themselves...


00:41:27.279 --> 00:41:29.213
...and each other.


00:41:30.382 --> 00:41:33.374
When times are tough,
people like to blame someone.


00:41:34.820 --> 00:41:37.050
And hobos are an easy target, I guess.


00:41:42.428 --> 00:41:43.861
Here we are.


00:41:46.098 --> 00:41:47.156
Bedroom.


00:41:47.333 --> 00:41:49.893
Uh, lounge.


00:41:50.069 --> 00:41:51.093
Bathroom.


00:41:53.606 --> 00:41:56.268
Library. Come on in.


00:41:56.842 --> 00:41:58.901
You actually sleep here?
- Hi, doc.


00:41:59.078 --> 00:42:00.340
Will.


00:42:00.512 --> 00:42:02.343
How's that throat of Countee's?


00:42:02.514 --> 00:42:05.608
- Oh, it's doing much better. Thank you.
- Good.


00:42:05.784 --> 00:42:07.308
Robin Hood, huh?


00:42:07.820 --> 00:42:08.946
Yeah.


00:42:09.121 --> 00:42:11.555
Hey, uh... Doc, what do
you think about a person...


00:42:11.724 --> 00:42:14.659
...who steals from the
rich and gives to the poor?


00:42:15.127 --> 00:42:17.891
Is he a good guy or a bad guy?


00:42:19.865 --> 00:42:21.628
I'd have to say a good one.


00:42:23.402 --> 00:42:24.926
Yeah, me too.


00:42:26.071 --> 00:42:27.595
See you, doc.


00:42:28.340 --> 00:42:29.671
Will.


00:42:30.242 --> 00:42:33.109
Hey. Y'all come out here. I
got someone to introduce you to.


00:42:33.279 --> 00:42:34.678
Okay. Okay.


00:42:34.847 --> 00:42:36.439
Welcome.


00:42:38.183 --> 00:42:40.981
Sheldon Pennington III.


00:42:42.354 --> 00:42:46.882
- Stirling Howard IV.
- Kit Kittredge I.


00:42:47.126 --> 00:42:48.354
I'm Ruthie.


00:42:48.527 --> 00:42:50.757
- We're friends of Will and Countee.
- Ah.


00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:54.366
Pennington's the name,
stocks and bonds were my game.


00:42:55.501 --> 00:42:57.366
My father used to be in stocks too.


00:42:57.536 --> 00:43:00.767
Ah. Operative words, "used to be. "


00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:05.639
You'll find a lot of men around here
who used to be one thing or another.


00:43:07.780 --> 00:43:10.010
When I acquire some
gainful employment...


00:43:10.182 --> 00:43:12.548
...I am going to send
for my wife and two sons.


00:43:12.718 --> 00:43:15.653
One of them is just about your age.


00:43:16.322 --> 00:43:17.687
Stirling Howard IV...


00:43:17.990 --> 00:43:19.457
...where have you been?


00:43:19.825 --> 00:43:22.123
With Will and Countee
at the hobo jungle.


00:43:25.531 --> 00:43:28.125
Don't you ever take him there
again, do you understand?


00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:29.528
Ever.


00:43:30.035 --> 00:43:31.525
Yes, ma'am.


00:43:31.937 --> 00:43:34.701
It wasn't his fault. Uh, uh...


00:43:34.873 --> 00:43:37.899
- I was just...
- I gave them permission, Louise.


00:43:38.744 --> 00:43:40.371
Excuse us.


00:43:40.713 --> 00:43:42.738
I warned you about spending
time with those hobos.


00:43:42.915 --> 00:43:45.145
Mom... A lot of them are criminals.


00:43:45.317 --> 00:43:48.548
- Next time, you get permission from me.
- I have to go.


00:43:49.054 --> 00:43:50.351
Come on.


00:43:50.522 --> 00:43:56.427
Ah. I'm sorry, Mother.
- Oh, you don't have to apologize.


00:43:58.030 --> 00:44:00.794
We just can't afford to antagonize
the boarders, that's all.


00:44:00.966 --> 00:44:02.695
Here, sit with me.


00:44:05.871 --> 00:44:08.135
I never wanted this for you.


00:44:09.975 --> 00:44:11.602
Should be...


00:44:11.777 --> 00:44:17.044
...out playing, not worrying
about boarders and mortgages...


00:44:18.017 --> 00:44:19.109
...and selling eggs.


00:44:19.284 --> 00:44:20.410
Selling what?


00:44:20.586 --> 00:44:23.919
- Uh, eggs.
- But we don't have any chickens.


00:44:24.089 --> 00:44:26.990
Yes, we do. Aunt Millie sent them.


00:44:27.326 --> 00:44:29.055
Kit, it will help us.


00:44:31.663 --> 00:44:33.130
- Oh, no.
- Kit.


00:44:33.298 --> 00:44:34.925
- No.
- Kit.


00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:38.501
Everyone knows that selling eggs
is one step away from the poorhouse.


00:44:38.670 --> 00:44:39.967
Margaret Mildred, sit down.


00:44:42.441 --> 00:44:43.601
Look at me.


00:44:43.776 --> 00:44:47.212
Selling eggs, growing vegetables,
reusing everything we can...


00:44:47.379 --> 00:44:50.177
...may be the only thing
to keep us out of poorhouse.


00:44:50.349 --> 00:44:53.614
And it's either that or go
to live with Uncle Hendrick.


00:44:54.186 --> 00:44:56.620
Until your father finds work.


00:44:57.856 --> 00:44:59.323
So, what'll it be?


00:45:00.092 --> 00:45:01.616
The chickens or the uncle?


00:45:03.162 --> 00:45:04.959
Hmm?


00:45:05.464 --> 00:45:06.726
I'll take the chickens.


00:45:09.101 --> 00:45:10.728
Thank you, thank you.


00:45:10.903 --> 00:45:14.737
Thank you. Thank you.
I, Jefferson J. Berk...


00:45:14.907 --> 00:45:17.273
...will suspend a person in midair.


00:45:18.010 --> 00:45:19.272
Levitation?


00:45:19.445 --> 00:45:22.243
Mm-hm. No trickery, no distractions.


00:45:22.581 --> 00:45:27.484
Right under your noses, I will
make someone in this room float.


00:45:29.088 --> 00:45:30.715
Weightless.


00:45:32.324 --> 00:45:33.621
I need a volunteer.


00:45:36.328 --> 00:45:38.091
Miss Bond?


00:45:39.298 --> 00:45:42.324
I have never levitated a
mobile librarian before.


00:45:42.801 --> 00:45:44.792
Have you ever levitated
a dance instructor?


00:45:45.237 --> 00:45:47.102
Yes, but that's a long story.


00:45:47.272 --> 00:45:48.967
- Come, Miss Bond.
- Oh, no.


00:45:49.141 --> 00:45:51.006
Yes.
- No, Mr. Berk, I couldn't.


00:45:51.176 --> 00:45:52.336
Yeah. Come on.


00:45:52.511 --> 00:45:53.773
There you go.
- Yeah.


00:45:53.946 --> 00:45:56.642
Miss Bond, Miss Bond, Miss Bond.


00:45:57.983 --> 00:45:59.951
Oh!


00:46:00.119 --> 00:46:02.713
- All right, but it won't
work. Please sit down.


00:46:02.888 --> 00:46:04.981
All right. Lean back.


00:46:07.059 --> 00:46:09.994
All right. That's it, very good.


00:46:10.162 --> 00:46:13.996
Cross your arms over your
chest. Good. Close your eyes.


00:46:14.166 --> 00:46:17.260
Close your eyes. Close your eyes.


00:46:17.736 --> 00:46:19.363
- Are you comfortable?
- No.


00:46:19.538 --> 00:46:21.472
Excellent. Children, the lights, please?


00:46:23.842 --> 00:46:25.810
Miss Dooley, would you accompany me?


00:46:25.978 --> 00:46:27.377
I do.


00:46:27.546 --> 00:46:29.036
Will, yes.


00:46:48.033 --> 00:46:49.557
Miss Bond.


00:46:49.735 --> 00:46:52.260
You are getting sleepy.


00:46:52.437 --> 00:46:54.428
Very sleepy.


00:46:54.606 --> 00:46:56.506
You're feeling as light as air.


00:46:56.675 --> 00:46:59.610
And you only hear my
voice. Only my voice.


00:46:59.778 --> 00:47:02.372
All other sounds fade away.


00:47:02.848 --> 00:47:07.285
Your eyelids are getting heavy, very
heavy. You can barely keep them open.


00:47:08.086 --> 00:47:09.678
You...


00:47:09.988 --> 00:47:11.717
...are now...


00:47:13.025 --> 00:47:14.049
...asleep.


00:47:15.294 --> 00:47:17.285
- Ah!
- Shh.


00:47:17.462 --> 00:47:20.260
Please, Mrs. Howard, any
loud noise could awaken her...


00:47:20.432 --> 00:47:22.024
...and she could fall.


00:47:55.834 --> 00:47:57.301
Shh!


00:49:11.510 --> 00:49:12.534
Yeah, yeah.


00:49:12.711 --> 00:49:14.736
Miss Bond, ladies and gentlemen.


00:49:16.315 --> 00:49:18.306
Well, I told you it wouldn't work.


00:49:21.286 --> 00:49:26.189
Mother, Mother, you should have seen
it. Mr. Berk just levitated Miss Bond.


00:49:26.825 --> 00:49:29.157
Well, are you all right?


00:49:29.328 --> 00:49:31.819
Of course I am because nothing happened.


00:49:31.997 --> 00:49:33.589
- Yes, it did.
- Oh, no.


00:49:33.765 --> 00:49:35.027
- You were in the air.
- No.


00:49:35.200 --> 00:49:36.599
- Is that the mail?
- Yes.


00:49:36.768 --> 00:49:40.226
- Uh, excuse me for a moment.
- Uh, but the mail's here, Mother.


00:49:40.405 --> 00:49:42.600
- Mr. Berk.
- Thank you.


00:49:42.774 --> 00:49:44.002
- Miss Bond.
- Ooh!


00:49:44.176 --> 00:49:46.610
- Is there one from Dad?
- No, sweetie, not today.


00:49:47.312 --> 00:49:49.280
Aw. He writes often enough.


00:49:49.448 --> 00:49:52.940
I must say, Mrs. K., if
I marry, when I marry...


00:49:53.118 --> 00:49:55.643
...I hope my husband is
half as attentive as yours.


00:49:55.821 --> 00:49:58.756
My luck, I'll probably get
some deadbeat who never...


00:49:59.291 --> 00:50:01.782
Mind. Never mind.


00:50:01.960 --> 00:50:05.418
- Oh, no. Oh, no.
- Is anything wrong?


00:50:05.797 --> 00:50:10.632
Oh, my poor cousin Frederich,
he was jumped by a hobo.


00:50:11.136 --> 00:50:13.434
Took his watch, his wallet...


00:50:13.605 --> 00:50:14.936
...and beat him pretty badly.


00:50:15.107 --> 00:50:16.768
Oh. What a shame.


00:50:16.942 --> 00:50:18.432
Is his wife looking after him?


00:50:20.045 --> 00:50:21.910
- No, he's not married.
- Oh.


00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:24.640
In fact, I'm the only family he has.


00:50:24.816 --> 00:50:28.377
Mrs. Kittredge, would you mind if he
came and stayed with us for a few days?


00:50:28.553 --> 00:50:32.683
I mean, when he gets better. I
promise, I will pay extra for his meals.


00:50:32.858 --> 00:50:35.452
Of course, I have a
spare cot in the attic.


00:50:35.627 --> 00:50:37.652
- I'll get it ready for you.
- Thank you.


00:50:37.829 --> 00:50:40.593
Miss Bond and I were just
talking about these hobo crimes.


00:50:40.766 --> 00:50:44.600
Well, it's gossip, really. You hear
these things when you're mobile.


00:50:44.770 --> 00:50:49.298
But they say there's been
a slew of hobo robberies.


00:50:49.474 --> 00:50:53.911
- Well, not all hobos are alike.
- They've been ransacking people's homes...


00:50:54.079 --> 00:50:57.537
...and mugging them in broad daylight.
Really, something should be done.


00:50:57.716 --> 00:50:59.980
Well, there's talk of
running them out of town.


00:51:00.986 --> 00:51:02.749
They are ransacking people's homes.


00:51:02.921 --> 00:51:05.788
I wonder if it's not a bad idea
if we don't lock up our valuables.


00:51:05.957 --> 00:51:08.050
I sew my money into my unmentionables.


00:51:08.226 --> 00:51:10.694
Oh, Miss Dooley, please,
there are children here.


00:51:10.862 --> 00:51:13.422
Don't they wear unmentionables?
I certainly hope so.


00:51:15.167 --> 00:51:17.431
My father keeps our money
in the basement safe.


00:51:17.602 --> 00:51:21.800
Ha! Isn't that a kicker? The
banker who doesn't trust the bank.


00:51:21.973 --> 00:51:25.773
Mrs. K., would you mind
keeping that in your lockbox?


00:51:25.944 --> 00:51:28.344
It's old and, they say, quite valuable.


00:51:28.914 --> 00:51:30.609
Just in case.


00:51:30.949 --> 00:51:32.712
Of course. It's beautiful.


00:51:32.884 --> 00:51:34.852
Uh, yes?


00:51:36.555 --> 00:51:39.149
Just wanna tell Ms. Kittredge I
found a place to put that coop...


00:51:39.324 --> 00:51:42.020
...over in the north corner
by the, uh, clothesline there.


00:51:42.194 --> 00:51:45.027
Excellent. Thank you, Will.
- Come on, Countee.


00:51:46.665 --> 00:51:47.689
Good day.


00:51:47.866 --> 00:51:49.629
- Bye, Will. Bye, Countee.
- Bye.


00:51:49.801 --> 00:51:52.167
I think perhaps I should
lock these up as well.


00:51:52.337 --> 00:51:54.828
Yes, I think I might follow suit.


00:51:55.006 --> 00:51:58.703
I should hate to lose my father's watch.


00:52:01.980 --> 00:52:04.244
And these rings as well.


00:52:06.585 --> 00:52:08.576
But that's your wedding ring.


00:52:08.753 --> 00:52:12.951
It's okay. It's just for
safekeeping, Stirling.


00:52:15.927 --> 00:52:19.658
"Kindness and Honor
in the Hobo Community"?


00:52:20.499 --> 00:52:23.559
There's no question
mark. It's a declaration.


00:52:23.735 --> 00:52:26.499
"Kindness and Honor in
the Hobo Community. "


00:52:26.671 --> 00:52:29.037
You've got to admit, it's fresh.


00:52:29.207 --> 00:52:30.936
Yes, but the thing is, sweetheart...


00:52:31.243 --> 00:52:36.772
...you generally want people to
buy the newspaper, not laugh at it.


00:52:36.948 --> 00:52:39.212
There are good hobos and bad ones, sir.


00:52:39.584 --> 00:52:41.518
Just like with apples.


00:52:41.920 --> 00:52:43.512
And with...


00:52:43.989 --> 00:52:45.081
...editors.


00:52:47.125 --> 00:52:48.752
Come here, Kit.


00:52:49.861 --> 00:52:51.453
See that map?


00:52:53.098 --> 00:52:56.397
Every one of those cities,
Dayton, Hamilton, Cincinnati...


00:52:56.568 --> 00:52:59.560
...has reported crimes where
hobos are the prime suspects.


00:52:59.738 --> 00:53:03.196
They're offering $ 150 to anyone
who catches the hobo criminals.


00:53:03.375 --> 00:53:07.744
So ask yourself, who's gonna pay a
nickel to hear what good folks hobos are?


00:53:08.146 --> 00:53:10.637
That's exactly why
they should hear it now.


00:53:10.815 --> 00:53:12.214
Let me give you a tip.


00:53:12.384 --> 00:53:15.820
Sometimes you gotta play the tune
your audience wants to sing to, Kit.


00:53:16.955 --> 00:53:19.480
There's been a burglary.
51 North Abbott Place.


00:53:20.158 --> 00:53:24.424
- Got an ID on a hobo with a limp.
- That's where Ruthie lives.


00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:44.846
It was a hobo. Jumped
right out in front of me.


00:53:45.016 --> 00:53:46.313
Right out in broad daylight.


00:53:46.484 --> 00:53:48.111
Had a limp too. Yes.


00:53:48.286 --> 00:53:50.618
- Anything else?
- Did he have a tattoo?


00:53:50.789 --> 00:53:52.916
Something unusual, like a bird, maybe?


00:53:53.091 --> 00:53:56.857
- Okay, this is a crime scene, miss.
- Yeah, actually, he did have something.


00:53:57.028 --> 00:53:59.428
- Uh, bright colors.
- That's it.


00:53:59.598 --> 00:54:02.089
That's the tattoo. Frank?


00:54:02.267 --> 00:54:03.325
Over here.


00:54:09.341 --> 00:54:14.176
- What do you make of that, sheriff?
That I'd say is a calling card.


00:54:16.414 --> 00:54:17.506
Will?


00:54:18.016 --> 00:54:19.916
Will?


00:54:22.754 --> 00:54:25.314
- Everything all right, Kit?
- Where's Will?


00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:28.017
I don't know. He was
supposed to be here.


00:54:28.426 --> 00:54:30.155
Maybe he got held up.


00:54:30.328 --> 00:54:31.454
Mail call.


00:54:31.630 --> 00:54:34.064
Ah. If it was only that easy.


00:54:34.232 --> 00:54:39.192
Let's see, we have one for Mr.
Berk. I'll give that to him later.


00:54:39.604 --> 00:54:40.969
Of course, Mrs. Kittredge.


00:54:42.774 --> 00:54:44.401
From Dad?


00:54:46.711 --> 00:54:49.202
Uh, it's from Aunt Millie.


00:54:49.381 --> 00:54:51.144
And, Stirling, there's one for you.


00:54:53.918 --> 00:54:55.283
Mother.


00:54:55.453 --> 00:54:56.886
It's from New York.


00:54:57.422 --> 00:54:58.719
From Dad.


00:55:04.162 --> 00:55:06.756
And there's one in here for you too.


00:55:16.574 --> 00:55:18.166
Forty dollars?


00:55:20.712 --> 00:55:22.577
Um...


00:55:23.748 --> 00:55:25.375
Excuse me.


00:55:44.669 --> 00:55:45.897
There you are.


00:55:46.071 --> 00:55:47.333
What are you doing up here?


00:55:47.505 --> 00:55:50.099
- Sorry, I was just...
- No, no, it's fine.


00:55:50.275 --> 00:55:52.743
I'm just changing the sheets.


00:55:55.313 --> 00:55:58.578
I was looking for you earlier. I
forgot to ask you about the letter.


00:55:58.750 --> 00:56:01.878
Does your father say when
he's coming back to Cincinnati?


00:56:05.990 --> 00:56:07.321
Stirling?


00:56:12.897 --> 00:56:14.057
Oh, no.


00:56:14.532 --> 00:56:17.330
He wants you to move
to New York, doesn't he?


00:56:19.938 --> 00:56:22.839
That's it. That's why you're so upset.


00:56:24.576 --> 00:56:26.806
Your father found a job in New York...


00:56:26.978 --> 00:56:29.310
...and now he wants
you to move with him.


00:56:30.315 --> 00:56:33.250
Why can't people find
jobs in Cincinnati?


00:56:34.686 --> 00:56:36.847
I hate the mailman.


00:56:37.021 --> 00:56:38.613
And mailing...


00:56:38.790 --> 00:56:40.849
...letters and words. I hate words.


00:56:41.025 --> 00:56:44.586
- What are you talking about?
- Because you believe them.


00:56:45.163 --> 00:56:46.994
Even when they're not true.


00:56:52.070 --> 00:56:55.198
He said he'd write. He
said, "You'll hear from me. "


00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:57.837
Your father?


00:56:58.042 --> 00:56:59.907
I made him promise.


00:57:00.278 --> 00:57:01.802
You did?


00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:04.043
He kept his word at first.


00:57:04.916 --> 00:57:07.214
I got a letter every week.


00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:11.448
Then they got fewer and fewer...


00:57:11.623 --> 00:57:14.251
...until one day, they just stopped.


00:57:16.961 --> 00:57:18.952
And then she took her ring off.


00:57:23.735 --> 00:57:26.363
You wrote that letter to your mother?


00:57:28.540 --> 00:57:30.838
She just needs to hear from him.


00:57:32.644 --> 00:57:34.874
But there was money inside.


00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:41.648
He gave it to me before he left.


00:57:43.454 --> 00:57:45.513
For an emergency.


00:57:48.493 --> 00:57:51.326
I think I always sort of knew.


00:57:54.599 --> 00:57:57.159
He's never coming back to us.


00:58:58.496 --> 00:59:00.123
Hi, Kit.


00:59:01.466 --> 00:59:03.764
I got something for you.


00:59:12.777 --> 00:59:14.472
It's a scooter.


00:59:15.813 --> 00:59:16.837
Thank you.


00:59:17.849 --> 00:59:20.374
Will, what happened to your leg?


00:59:20.551 --> 00:59:21.848
I hurt it the other night.


00:59:22.020 --> 00:59:25.080
Are you sure it didn't
happen yesterday morning?


00:59:28.593 --> 00:59:31.084
Do you mind if I ask
you a few questions, son?


00:59:31.262 --> 00:59:32.752
No, sir.


00:59:35.867 --> 00:59:38.927
How exactly did you hurt your leg?


00:59:40.338 --> 00:59:41.362
I was jumped.


00:59:42.006 --> 00:59:43.803
Is that so?


00:59:44.642 --> 00:59:46.041
Yes, sir.


00:59:46.210 --> 00:59:48.007
It was the other night.


00:59:48.179 --> 00:59:50.170
Someone jumped me and
kicked me in the knee.


00:59:51.783 --> 00:59:53.978
Are those the boots you were wearing?


00:59:56.921 --> 01:00:00.618
- No, he took my boots.
- Now, isn't that a shame?


01:00:00.959 --> 01:00:04.360
Now, if we wanted to identify a
footprint, we'd be hard-pressed.


01:00:06.764 --> 01:00:09.198
Did you remember to report it?


01:00:10.601 --> 01:00:13.399
- Being a hobo, sir, who's gonna listen?
- Hmm.


01:00:14.205 --> 01:00:15.672
Do you know the Smithens?


01:00:17.508 --> 01:00:19.567
Ruthie Smithens?


01:00:21.012 --> 01:00:26.006
All these people seem to think
you know quite a bit about her.


01:00:26.818 --> 01:00:30.015
- About her financial situation.
- No. I don't know.


01:00:30.188 --> 01:00:33.214
Did you or did you not know
the Smithens were out of town?


01:00:35.893 --> 01:00:37.861
I knew they were gone, yes, sir.


01:00:38.029 --> 01:00:39.690
Do you remember her telling you...


01:00:39.864 --> 01:00:42.731
...they kept their money
in a safe in the basement?


01:00:44.302 --> 01:00:47.499
I'm gonna have to ask
you to remain local, son.


01:00:48.006 --> 01:00:51.134
You flip a train in the
middle of the night...


01:00:51.309 --> 01:00:54.335
...or you step one toe
outside the city limits...


01:00:54.512 --> 01:00:57.242
...I will track you down
and lock you up so fast...


01:00:57.415 --> 01:01:00.179
...you'll put those boots
right back on your feet.


01:01:00.351 --> 01:01:02.080
You understand me?


01:01:05.857 --> 01:01:06.915
Stop! Thief!


01:01:07.759 --> 01:01:11.889
- Hey, you! Stop!
- Hello?


01:01:12.196 --> 01:01:14.096
Mother? Are you there?


01:01:14.265 --> 01:01:15.892
Kit? Stirling? Who's there?


01:01:16.067 --> 01:01:19.195
What's going on? We've been robbed!


01:01:19.370 --> 01:01:21.600
One more time, Mr. Berk.


01:01:21.906 --> 01:01:23.373
I couldn't sleep.


01:01:23.541 --> 01:01:27.409
- I heard a noise, I thought, outside.
- So did I.


01:01:28.012 --> 01:01:31.072
So I went to check, and
that was when I saw him.


01:01:31.249 --> 01:01:32.443
Will Shepherd?


01:01:32.850 --> 01:01:33.908
It was dark.


01:01:34.719 --> 01:01:37.882
It was Will. And he was
carrying the lockbox.


01:01:39.490 --> 01:01:42.391
Ms. Kittredge, any of your
belongings in that lockbox?


01:01:43.227 --> 01:01:44.353
Um...


01:01:44.529 --> 01:01:46.724
...every penny we had
was in that lockbox.


01:01:47.265 --> 01:01:50.098
- He took your money?
- Of course he took their money.


01:01:50.268 --> 01:01:52.395
Knowing they needed it for the mortgage?


01:01:53.671 --> 01:01:55.798
- Let's pick him up, Frank.
- No.


01:01:58.443 --> 01:01:59.967
What a horrible boy.


01:02:01.312 --> 01:02:03.837
He talked a lot about Robin Hood, Kit.


01:02:04.015 --> 01:02:05.744
About stealing from the rich.


01:02:05.917 --> 01:02:09.318
But we're not rich, and he knows that.


01:02:09.487 --> 01:02:11.819
I don't believe it. I
just don't believe it.


01:02:12.590 --> 01:02:16.617
Will Shepherd? Has
anyone seen Will Shepherd?


01:02:17.328 --> 01:02:19.159
Will Shepherd?


01:02:31.209 --> 01:02:33.677
I'm sorry to bother you so early, ma'am.


01:02:33.978 --> 01:02:37.106
No, it's fine. Come in, please.


01:02:46.657 --> 01:02:48.955
- Morning.
- Morning.


01:02:49.727 --> 01:02:53.026
My deputy found these in Will
Shepherd's tent early this morning.


01:02:53.331 --> 01:02:55.561
We were hoping you could
ID them for us, ma'am.


01:02:57.535 --> 01:03:00.129
Are these the boots you
gave to Will Shepherd?


01:03:07.278 --> 01:03:08.609
And this?


01:03:09.080 --> 01:03:11.844
Oh, that's my brooch. Yes.


01:03:12.984 --> 01:03:15.817
Did...? Did you happen
to see my mother's ring?


01:03:16.420 --> 01:03:17.682
No, son.


01:03:17.855 --> 01:03:19.982
We figured he started
at the Smithens' house...


01:03:20.158 --> 01:03:22.786
...then moved on to yours.
It's an inside job, all right.


01:03:22.960 --> 01:03:24.257
No, it's impossible.


01:03:24.529 --> 01:03:27.054
The man who robbed the
Smithens' house had a tattoo.


01:03:27.231 --> 01:03:30.530
I heard it from an
eyewitness. I have notes.


01:03:30.701 --> 01:03:33.932
- Will doesn't have a tattoo, sheriff.
- Sweetheart.


01:03:34.105 --> 01:03:35.732
Have you found him yet?


01:03:36.774 --> 01:03:38.765
Nobody's seen him.


01:03:38.943 --> 01:03:41.741
Nobody seems to know where he is.


01:03:43.748 --> 01:03:45.613
Dear Dad, it's been weeks...


01:03:45.783 --> 01:03:48.274
...and there's still no
sign of Will or Countee.


01:03:48.452 --> 01:03:50.477
I just don't believe he'd rob us, Dad.


01:03:50.655 --> 01:03:52.919
But someone did, and
now our money's all gone.


01:03:53.090 --> 01:03:57.424
Now there's a foreclosure sign in the
middle of our beautiful, beautiful lawn.


01:03:57.595 --> 01:04:01.292
If that's not bad enough, Uncle Hendrick
said we can't bring Grace with us...


01:04:01.465 --> 01:04:04.662
...when we go to live with him.
We have to give her away, Dad.


01:04:04.835 --> 01:04:07.065
How can I give her away?


01:04:07.238 --> 01:04:10.969
I wanna ask Mother to talk to
him again. She's so sad, Dad.


01:04:11.142 --> 01:04:13.406
She hardly ever smiles.


01:04:13.578 --> 01:04:15.068
Dad, why aren't you here?


01:04:15.246 --> 01:04:18.477
Why haven't we heard from you? You
promised you'd write every week...


01:04:18.649 --> 01:04:21.777
...and now it just keeps getting
longer and longer between letters.


01:04:21.953 --> 01:04:23.511
You can't disappear.


01:04:23.688 --> 01:04:26.350
If you're not working in Chicago,
why can't you not work in Cin...?


01:04:58.322 --> 01:05:01.689
Dear Dad, I got a letter from
Frances and Florence yesterday.


01:05:01.859 --> 01:05:05.351
They're settled with family in
Sacramento, California, doing great...


01:05:05.529 --> 01:05:07.326
...just like you said.


01:05:07.498 --> 01:05:10.262
Did Mother mention that Ruthie's
father spoke to the bank?


01:05:10.434 --> 01:05:13.198
And they're giving us an extra
month to stay in the house?


01:05:13.371 --> 01:05:16.670
I know that Ruthie pushed for
us. She's such a true friend.


01:05:17.341 --> 01:05:20.799
Last week, Mother made a dress
for me out of a chicken-feed sack.


01:05:20.978 --> 01:05:23.378
I wore it on the first day of
school. I didn't mind a bit.


01:05:23.547 --> 01:05:24.673
It's so beautiful.


01:05:24.849 --> 01:05:27.750
In fact, a few of the girls
thought it looked so smart...


01:05:27.918 --> 01:05:30.853
...she made some for
them too. For money.


01:05:31.022 --> 01:05:32.284
Imagine that.


01:05:32.456 --> 01:05:34.856
All that feed-sack material
was being thrown away.


01:05:35.026 --> 01:05:36.425
That's pure profit.


01:05:36.594 --> 01:05:40.826
Aunt Millie will be so proud, and
Uncle Hendrick will be horrified.


01:05:40.998 --> 01:05:44.058
I think I'll wear it the
first day we move in with him.


01:05:45.236 --> 01:05:47.864
I know your legs are
short, but you can do it.


01:05:48.039 --> 01:05:49.506
Yes, good girl, good girl.


01:05:49.674 --> 01:05:53.405
And selling eggs isn't as bad as I
thought. In fact, it's kind of fun.


01:05:53.678 --> 01:05:58.342
Eggs! Get your eggs! Fresh
eggs. Fifteen cents a dozen.


01:05:58.516 --> 01:06:00.848
Get your eggs. Fresh eggs.


01:06:01.018 --> 01:06:04.545
Mr. Berk went to pick up his cousin
Frederich from the train station.


01:06:04.722 --> 01:06:07.213
I think a new face in the
house will cheer us all up.


01:06:07.391 --> 01:06:09.291
And wherever Will and
Countee are, I only hope...


01:06:17.034 --> 01:06:19.025
Is he wearing the diaper?


01:06:19.203 --> 01:06:21.330
Yes, Jefferson.


01:06:22.406 --> 01:06:23.737
A monkey?


01:06:28.045 --> 01:06:31.344
His name's Curtis. Hmm. Heh.


01:06:33.351 --> 01:06:38.015
I love you, Curtis. Who's
my little monkey boy?


01:06:47.865 --> 01:06:49.696
He's so cute.


01:06:53.270 --> 01:06:57.366
Stirling, get away from
that hideous creature now.


01:06:57.541 --> 01:06:58.940
Oh, oh, oh!


01:06:59.110 --> 01:07:01.044
Get it off. Get it off. Off, off, off!


01:07:05.282 --> 01:07:06.874
Curtis, come.


01:07:08.352 --> 01:07:09.751
Scratch his little tummy...


01:07:10.121 --> 01:07:12.248
...and he'll be your
friend forever, won't you?


01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:14.457
If I scratch his...


01:07:14.859 --> 01:07:15.951
Margaret.


01:07:16.327 --> 01:07:19.785
It's a good thing we're moving
because otherwise, we'd move.


01:07:23.167 --> 01:07:26.830
Uh, Mr. Berk, there's a cot
ready for you upstairs now.


01:07:27.004 --> 01:07:30.269
I'm sorry for the mess. It's
such a large house to pack up.


01:07:30.441 --> 01:07:32.033
There's no time to do it.


01:07:32.209 --> 01:07:35.838
Thanks for your trouble, ma'am.
I'm only staying a few days.


01:07:36.013 --> 01:07:39.278
- Are you from Baltimore too, Mr. Berk?
- Call me Frederich.


01:07:40.618 --> 01:07:43.451
Originally, I'm from Canada.
- What a coincidence.


01:07:44.088 --> 01:07:46.921
- I thought you were from Poughkeepsie.
- Shh!


01:07:47.091 --> 01:07:50.959
Uh, but I moved to Baltimore in '22
to work with Jeff in the magic show.


01:07:51.128 --> 01:07:54.791
We called ourselves Luigi and Sam.


01:07:55.366 --> 01:07:57.266
I was Luigi.


01:07:57.668 --> 01:08:00.330
Yeah. Good times.


01:08:00.504 --> 01:08:01.801
Anyway.


01:08:01.972 --> 01:08:04.702
Jeff would saw a woman
in half while Lola sang.


01:08:05.075 --> 01:08:07.976
- Lola?
- Yeah.


01:08:09.079 --> 01:08:12.776
Lola. This was her favorite spot.


01:08:13.017 --> 01:08:15.542
May she rest in peace.


01:08:16.253 --> 01:08:18.983
The sheriff said it was an inside job.


01:08:19.156 --> 01:08:21.647
So Mr. Berk knew where Mother
kept the jewels and money.


01:08:21.826 --> 01:08:25.853
But how did Mr. Berk get the brooch
out of the lockbox and into Will's tent?


01:08:26.030 --> 01:08:30.467
What if he never put
the brooch in the box?


01:08:30.634 --> 01:08:33.159
That's it. A sleight of hand.


01:08:33.337 --> 01:08:36.534
Remember when Mr. Berk put
his watch in the jewelry bag?


01:08:36.707 --> 01:08:39.972
He must taken the brooch
out, put it in his pocket...


01:08:40.144 --> 01:08:41.839
- And given it to...
- Frederich.


01:08:42.012 --> 01:08:43.912
He must have stolen Will's boots.


01:08:44.081 --> 01:08:48.017
Right. And that means that Frederich
must have gone to Will's camp...


01:08:48.185 --> 01:08:51.985
...dressed as a hobo and hid the
boots and jewelry in Will's tent.


01:08:52.623 --> 01:08:55.922
All Mr. Berk needed to do was
to make the sheriff suspect Will.


01:08:56.594 --> 01:08:58.027
The limp.


01:08:59.129 --> 01:09:01.290
Will said someone
kicked him in the knee.


01:09:01.465 --> 01:09:05.162
They made him limp and
then committed a crime...


01:09:05.336 --> 01:09:07.531
...where a limping hobo
could be identified.


01:09:08.138 --> 01:09:11.835
- At my house. That's why
he did it in broad daylight.


01:09:12.009 --> 01:09:13.874
He left the boot print in the mud...


01:09:14.311 --> 01:09:17.610
...and wanted someone
to see him limp away.


01:09:18.716 --> 01:09:19.978
What's going on over there?


01:09:21.785 --> 01:09:24.686
We figured it out, Kit. But
now we have to go to the police.


01:09:24.855 --> 01:09:25.981
No, not without proof.


01:09:27.491 --> 01:09:29.459
Do you know where? Of course.


01:09:29.627 --> 01:09:30.685
I certainly hope so.


01:09:32.296 --> 01:09:33.354
Perfect.


01:09:33.531 --> 01:09:36.125
Let's go look in their
room for the money.


01:09:41.372 --> 01:09:43.397
If anyone comes back, blow this whistle.


01:09:43.908 --> 01:09:45.170
Are you sure about this?


01:09:45.342 --> 01:09:47.833
Look, how often is the
house completely empty?


01:09:48.012 --> 01:09:50.981
Everyone's gone. It's now or never.


01:09:55.019 --> 01:09:56.509
Come on.


01:10:05.229 --> 01:10:07.288
- Ruthie, keep watch.
- Okay.


01:10:14.371 --> 01:10:19.604
We need to find our money. Where
is it? It's all the money we have.


01:10:20.678 --> 01:10:21.872
In mystery novels...


01:10:22.046 --> 01:10:24.708
...there's usually a button
on the side of these trunks...


01:10:24.882 --> 01:10:26.850
...that opens a secret compartment.


01:10:27.017 --> 01:10:29.952
Where is it? Where's that button?


01:10:34.291 --> 01:10:35.519
Ruthie, I found it.


01:10:39.697 --> 01:10:40.857
This is the red wallet.


01:10:42.733 --> 01:10:45.167
Thought you said you had a map. I do.


01:10:45.369 --> 01:10:46.700
I have one.


01:10:46.870 --> 01:10:49.464
- I just don't remember where
exactly. Oh, that's great.


01:10:49.640 --> 01:10:53.132
- You need a map to find the map, right?
- I've got a map, Jeff.


01:10:55.546 --> 01:10:58.276
Ruthie, your mother's necklace.


01:10:58.449 --> 01:11:00.178
But where's the lockbox?


01:11:03.053 --> 01:11:05.021
Look at this.


01:11:05.556 --> 01:11:09.151
Dayton. Cincinnati. Hamilton.


01:11:12.129 --> 01:11:13.790
Sarnia? No.


01:11:15.265 --> 01:11:16.527
Columbus.


01:11:16.934 --> 01:11:19.903
Cleveland. Ruthie, do
you know what this means?


01:11:20.237 --> 01:11:21.727
Yes.


01:11:21.905 --> 01:11:23.634
- No.
- Mr. Berk's cousin...


01:11:23.807 --> 01:11:26.503
...has been in every city
where the hobo crimes happened.


01:11:27.211 --> 01:11:30.146
Holy cow.


01:11:30.447 --> 01:11:32.608
Oh, my gosh. Where'd this come from?


01:11:32.783 --> 01:11:33.875
Stirling.


01:11:34.351 --> 01:11:37.320
The hobo sign for danger.


01:11:42.092 --> 01:11:43.719
Shh!


01:11:46.864 --> 01:11:49.833
Make him stop that squealing.
Put him in that thing.


01:11:51.735 --> 01:11:56.536
- He doesn't react well to tension.
- Neither do I. You don't hear me screaming!


01:11:56.807 --> 01:11:59.401
All right, just give me
the map. Come on, let's go.


01:12:00.411 --> 01:12:02.311
What are you doing?


01:12:05.716 --> 01:12:07.274
There is no map.


01:12:07.451 --> 01:12:11.547
Right? You buried the loot, but you
have no idea exactly where you buried it.


01:12:11.722 --> 01:12:14.282
Let me think, all right?


01:12:14.458 --> 01:12:17.518
- Just let me think.
- Yeah, well, that'll take forever.


01:12:17.695 --> 01:12:22.496
I put it somewhere I knew I wouldn't
forget, somewhere I was sure to find it.


01:12:29.339 --> 01:12:32.536
You see? I'm not the
numbskull you think I am.


01:12:32.710 --> 01:12:35.838
Okay, great. Let's just
get going, okay? Come on.


01:12:36.346 --> 01:12:38.473
Hey, what's this doing open?


01:12:38.649 --> 01:12:40.378
Is everything still there?


01:12:43.053 --> 01:12:45.283
- Where is it?
- Shh.


01:12:49.827 --> 01:12:52.921
You probably just left it open
by accident. Come on, let's go.


01:12:55.065 --> 01:12:57.124
Then what's this rock doing here?


01:12:57.501 --> 01:13:00.095
It probably came
from the out-of-doors.


01:13:00.938 --> 01:13:05.272
It's got Hobo writing on it,
Jefferson. It means danger.


01:13:05.743 --> 01:13:07.267
I don't like it.


01:13:08.212 --> 01:13:09.509
Not one little bit.


01:13:11.081 --> 01:13:13.276
All right, help me with this trunk.


01:13:13.450 --> 01:13:16.419
- Where are we going with my trunk?
- No, we are not going anywhere.


01:13:16.587 --> 01:13:18.054
That would just raise suspicion.


01:13:18.222 --> 01:13:20.349
You are gonna tell me exactly
where the loot is buried.


01:13:20.524 --> 01:13:22.651
Then you're on a 4:00
train to Baltimore.


01:13:22.826 --> 01:13:25.818
Bal...? By...? By myself?


01:13:26.130 --> 01:13:27.825
I hate traveling alone.


01:13:28.699 --> 01:13:33.693
Frederich, if your instinct is right,
and I highly, highly doubt it...


01:13:34.371 --> 01:13:38.467
...I don't want to have any nosy boarders
or ambitious little sheriffs sniffing around.


01:13:38.642 --> 01:13:40.473
It's too risky. Do you understand me?


01:13:40.644 --> 01:13:42.771
- So let's go.
- What about Curtis?


01:13:43.981 --> 01:13:46.279
I promise I'll bring him
to you next week, okay?


01:13:46.450 --> 01:13:50.614
- You won't forget he likes raisins?
- Can we just go, please?!


01:13:52.389 --> 01:13:53.856
Come on.


01:13:56.093 --> 01:13:57.287
Wait, my coat.


01:14:00.164 --> 01:14:01.893
It's right there on the chair.


01:14:03.033 --> 01:14:04.500
Come on.


01:14:07.337 --> 01:14:10.170
Come on, you have everything now? Yeah.


01:14:21.185 --> 01:14:23.380
It's okay. It's okay.


01:14:24.988 --> 01:14:27.548
Shh! Good monkey. I'm
getting in that car.


01:14:27.724 --> 01:14:29.055
What? Where are you going?


01:14:29.226 --> 01:14:32.024
- It's too dangerous. Stop.
- I need to get our money back.


01:14:32.196 --> 01:14:34.687
It's the only chance we
have to keep our home.


01:14:41.572 --> 01:14:43.767
Kit, no.


01:14:44.141 --> 01:14:45.631
Kit.


01:14:50.080 --> 01:14:52.275
Oh, my gosh. Kit, what are you doing?


01:14:57.721 --> 01:15:00.849
Stirling. Stirling,
wake up. Are you okay?


01:15:01.024 --> 01:15:02.651
Yeah.


01:15:03.260 --> 01:15:05.592
You and Ruthie get the
police. I'm going with them.


01:15:05.996 --> 01:15:07.486
What?


01:15:24.314 --> 01:15:27.750
Don't see why I gotta
go. Because you have to.


01:15:28.518 --> 01:15:30.645
Curtis. You can't take
a monkey on a train.


01:15:38.996 --> 01:15:40.588
I love you too, Curtis.


01:15:41.431 --> 01:15:43.160
Get in the car.


01:15:45.335 --> 01:15:48.133
You know where everything
is, right? Yeah, I know.


01:15:48.305 --> 01:15:49.465
Yeah, I know. Do you?


01:15:49.640 --> 01:15:51.369
Then where am I going?
Which way do I turn?


01:15:51.541 --> 01:15:53.509
Left here. Thanks for telling me.


01:15:53.677 --> 01:15:55.406
That's nice. Stop yelling at me!


01:15:55.579 --> 01:15:59.572
I'm not. I'm trying to get
information. I won't show you where.


01:15:59.750 --> 01:16:03.277
I know where it is, not you. Who's
smart now, Mr. Mustache-Bald-Head?


01:16:03.453 --> 01:16:05.148
Moron!


01:16:12.262 --> 01:16:13.786
Miss Bond. Miss Bond.


01:16:13.964 --> 01:16:16.899
What?
- Miss Bond. Stirling, come on.


01:16:17.067 --> 01:16:19.399
- Miss Bond, we need you.
- Miss Bond. Miss Bond.


01:16:19.569 --> 01:16:21.730
We really need you.
- What?


01:16:21.905 --> 01:16:23.133
Really.


01:16:23.307 --> 01:16:25.468
We found out that the
Berks stole the money.


01:16:25.642 --> 01:16:27.974
And Kit got in the back of the car.


01:16:28.145 --> 01:16:30.739
And they said something
about a train station.


01:16:30.914 --> 01:16:32.905
Get in the car. Get in the car.


01:16:33.083 --> 01:16:35.210
I'm going. I'll call the police.


01:16:44.594 --> 01:16:47.062
Whoa!


01:16:50.067 --> 01:16:53.696
Whoa! Are you all right back there?


01:16:55.906 --> 01:16:58.534
Kit should never have gone with them.


01:16:58.709 --> 01:17:02.167
- What train were they catching?
- The 4:00 to Baltimore.


01:17:02.346 --> 01:17:05.975
It sounded like the money was buried
in the woods next to the train station.


01:17:06.149 --> 01:17:08.140
Interesting.


01:17:08.685 --> 01:17:11.779
Well, we've got to get
there before they find Kit.


01:17:11.955 --> 01:17:13.479
Okay, hang on!


01:17:23.800 --> 01:17:27.065
Whoa, nelly!


01:17:27.337 --> 01:17:30.829
Eight, nine, 10.


01:17:32.909 --> 01:17:34.604
All right, we dig.


01:17:34.778 --> 01:17:36.746
What do you mean "we"?


01:17:40.283 --> 01:17:44.344
Three, two, one.


01:17:45.455 --> 01:17:47.047
I got it now.


01:17:47.424 --> 01:17:49.187
Seven.


01:17:50.827 --> 01:17:52.761
Now, this is it.


01:17:54.431 --> 01:17:56.023
Ow!


01:17:57.868 --> 01:17:59.358
Alrighty.


01:18:01.438 --> 01:18:02.700
Oh, Miss Bond.


01:18:10.580 --> 01:18:12.673
- Miss Bond, no.
- Shh.


01:18:12.849 --> 01:18:15.579
Now, don't you worry.
Everything's gonna be just fine.


01:18:15.752 --> 01:18:17.151
That's Mr. Berk's car.


01:18:17.320 --> 01:18:19.754
I've called the police.
They'll be here any minute.


01:18:19.923 --> 01:18:23.086
Now, stay put, all right?


01:18:35.439 --> 01:18:38.931
- Come on, Ruthie. Let's follow.
- But she said to stay put.


01:18:39.109 --> 01:18:40.599
Come on, hurry.


01:18:52.089 --> 01:18:53.181
There she is.


01:18:53.356 --> 01:18:55.586
- Kit.
- What are you doing here?


01:18:55.959 --> 01:18:58.621
- Did anyone see you?
- No. Miss Bond brought us.


01:18:58.795 --> 01:19:00.660
She's gonna help us. Look.


01:19:01.198 --> 01:19:02.722
Is that it?


01:19:03.767 --> 01:19:05.667
Easy, easy.


01:19:11.708 --> 01:19:13.699
- That's it. That's it.
- Shovel.


01:19:18.348 --> 01:19:19.713
Come on.


01:19:20.717 --> 01:19:22.378
Yes.


01:19:22.719 --> 01:19:25.210
- They found the box.
- This isn't fair.


01:19:25.388 --> 01:19:29.188
That's our box. That's our
money. That's all that we have.


01:19:29.359 --> 01:19:31.589
We'll get it back to
you, Kit. Don't worry.


01:19:31.761 --> 01:19:33.854
- Kit was in your car.
They're blaming Will.


01:19:34.030 --> 01:19:37.796
Miss Bond called the
police. They're on their way.


01:19:38.568 --> 01:19:43.369
- Wait, where did Miss Bond call from?
- Your house, why?


01:19:45.475 --> 01:19:48.569
Our phone doesn't work, Stirling.


01:19:48.745 --> 01:19:51.339
Oh, no. Oh, no.


01:19:51.615 --> 01:19:53.276
Miss Bond.


01:19:53.450 --> 01:19:56.180
Miss Bond was in on it all along.


01:19:58.722 --> 01:20:00.747
How could I have not have seen it?


01:20:00.924 --> 01:20:03.290
Of course, every magician
has an accomplice.


01:20:03.460 --> 01:20:06.827
- What are you talking about, woman?
- You can't really levitate someone.


01:20:06.997 --> 01:20:09.989
- Unless they're in on the trick.
- Exactly.


01:20:10.567 --> 01:20:12.364
What do we do now?


01:20:13.737 --> 01:20:16.672
Turn around and go back right this...


01:20:18.942 --> 01:20:20.034
Magic.


01:20:21.278 --> 01:20:24.076
Yoo-hoo! Over here.


01:20:24.247 --> 01:20:25.714
She's got a camera.


01:20:26.783 --> 01:20:28.546
Kit!


01:20:35.825 --> 01:20:38.259
Yoo-hoo! Over here.


01:20:39.029 --> 01:20:40.553
Ooh! Ooh!


01:20:43.033 --> 01:20:44.227
No.


01:20:44.401 --> 01:20:46.392
Get up. No, wait. Wait, wait, wait.


01:20:46.736 --> 01:20:49.398
He's taking the loot. Get him.


01:21:06.223 --> 01:21:07.884
Get him. Get him.


01:21:08.058 --> 01:21:11.721
It's all right, Stirling.
No one's gonna hurt you.


01:21:11.895 --> 01:21:14.728
Oh, no. Oh, no. Oh, no.


01:21:26.476 --> 01:21:28.774
Look, the hobo jungle.
We can hide there.


01:21:30.447 --> 01:21:32.108
I've got an idea.


01:21:36.353 --> 01:21:37.513
What are you doing?


01:21:38.221 --> 01:21:40.689
Giving us a head start.


01:21:50.100 --> 01:21:54.264
Oh, just let the
Kittredges keep their money.


01:21:54.437 --> 01:21:56.997
- Oh, stop it.
- It was never part of our plan.


01:21:57.173 --> 01:22:01.542
Okay, there was $250 in there. I
am not letting them keep nothing.


01:22:01.711 --> 01:22:03.804
You tell your girlfriend to button it.


01:22:03.980 --> 01:22:06.039
Quiet.
- Girlfriend?


01:22:06.216 --> 01:22:09.447
Oh, don't be so naive, Lucinda. This
is the way it's gonna be from now on.


01:22:11.221 --> 01:22:12.347
Oh, lovebirds.


01:22:14.124 --> 01:22:16.888
It's a good thing I read Hobo.


01:22:18.161 --> 01:22:19.822
This way.


01:22:21.398 --> 01:22:23.389
Come on. Come on. Come on.


01:22:36.913 --> 01:22:38.175
Okay, I think we're close.


01:22:40.817 --> 01:22:42.341
Kit? Will.


01:22:42.519 --> 01:22:44.987
- What are you all
doing? They're chasing us.


01:22:45.155 --> 01:22:47.419
We know who did it.
The Berks and Miss Bond.


01:22:47.590 --> 01:22:49.387
Berks? I always knew it wasn't you.


01:22:49.559 --> 01:22:51.186
They're after us. They set you up.


01:22:51.361 --> 01:22:52.521
Come on, let's go.


01:22:58.601 --> 01:23:00.262
- Oh, yeah, that's great.
- Oh!


01:23:00.437 --> 01:23:02.064
Great, huh?


01:23:02.372 --> 01:23:04.966
Boy, sure is a good thing you read Hobo.


01:23:06.476 --> 01:23:09.912
Come on, let's go. Come
on. Come on. Come on.


01:23:24.194 --> 01:23:26.321
Hi. Ahem. Excuse me.


01:23:26.496 --> 01:23:28.396
I was wondering if
somebody could help us.


01:23:28.565 --> 01:23:30.897
Uh, we just happened to
be picnicking nearby...


01:23:31.067 --> 01:23:34.525
...and, uh, it seems that
we have lost our children.


01:23:34.704 --> 01:23:36.069
Oh, I'm sorry to hear that.


01:23:36.239 --> 01:23:39.640
Yes. So, naturally, we thought
that they had wandered over here.


01:23:39.809 --> 01:23:43.108
Oh, naturally, naturally. Has
anyone seen any lost children?


01:23:43.279 --> 01:23:45.247
Mm-mm.


01:23:48.118 --> 01:23:49.517
No.


01:23:50.420 --> 01:23:53.514
Well, I, uh... I have, um...


01:23:54.124 --> 01:23:55.557
Let's see. Uh...


01:23:55.725 --> 01:23:58.558
Fifty, 70, 80.


01:23:59.129 --> 01:24:01.290
A hundred, uh, dollars...


01:24:01.464 --> 01:24:06.128
...that, uh, I'm sure would probably
buy a lot of food for that baby...


01:24:06.302 --> 01:24:09.396
...and, uh, be able to give
some of you a fresh start.


01:24:09.572 --> 01:24:11.335
So that you might once again become...


01:24:11.741 --> 01:24:14.505
...respectable members
of the community again.


01:24:14.677 --> 01:24:16.838
Ah. Respectable members
of the community?


01:24:17.313 --> 01:24:19.804
- Yes.
- Like you?


01:24:21.985 --> 01:24:23.282
- Achoo!
- Shh!


01:24:30.794 --> 01:24:34.161
Hello, my little angels. Get them.


01:24:37.167 --> 01:24:38.498
Help! Help!


01:24:38.668 --> 01:24:41.136
Help! That's my money! Give it back!


01:24:41.304 --> 01:24:43.465
Not so fast.


01:24:44.841 --> 01:24:45.967
Let go of me.


01:24:47.110 --> 01:24:49.601
Aah! No!


01:24:53.249 --> 01:24:54.978
Thank you, ma'am.


01:24:55.752 --> 01:24:59.085
- Anytime.
- Lucinda.


01:24:59.556 --> 01:25:01.183
The magic is gone.


01:25:05.595 --> 01:25:07.529
No. No.


01:25:07.697 --> 01:25:10.461
No. Unh! Ow.


01:25:12.068 --> 01:25:17.267
Forgive me, Kit. All
of you, I'm so sorry.


01:25:17.440 --> 01:25:19.340
Somehow we convinced ourselves...


01:25:19.509 --> 01:25:24.003
...that desperate times
justify desperate measures.


01:25:24.180 --> 01:25:27.081
Well, Jefferson, I was being naive.


01:25:28.251 --> 01:25:33.553
There we were gallivanting across
the country like Robin Hood...


01:25:33.723 --> 01:25:36.658
...stealing from the
rich to give to the poor.


01:25:37.126 --> 01:25:38.650
When technically...


01:25:38.828 --> 01:25:45.324
...all we really did was steal from
the rich and the poor and kept it.


01:25:47.637 --> 01:25:49.730
Really, that's what we did.


01:25:50.139 --> 01:25:51.970
And you...


01:25:52.408 --> 01:25:56.071
...Jefferson Jasper Renee Berk...


01:25:56.246 --> 01:25:58.510
Ha, ha! Renee?


01:26:00.083 --> 01:26:01.846
Are no Robin Hood.


01:26:04.287 --> 01:26:07.688
Mac, why don't we go to town, tell
the sheriff we solved the crime spree?


01:26:07.857 --> 01:26:09.586
Tell him it wasn't a hobo after all.


01:26:09.759 --> 01:26:11.727
Yeah. That's right.


01:26:12.395 --> 01:26:14.158
Hey, come on. Easy.


01:26:14.931 --> 01:26:17.764
Goodbye, my friends.
- Bye, Miss Bond.


01:26:19.235 --> 01:26:20.759
Where's Countee, Will?


01:26:21.404 --> 01:26:24.931
You know how they say if you wanna hide
something, you do it in plain sight?


01:26:25.108 --> 01:26:27.702
- Uh-uh.
- Yeah. No.


01:26:28.978 --> 01:26:30.673
Hey, Countee.


01:26:40.223 --> 01:26:42.817
You dressed him as a girl?
- I am a girl.


01:26:43.426 --> 01:26:45.291
He's a she? You're a her?


01:26:46.863 --> 01:26:50.060
Everyone, meet Constance
Angel Eudora Garvey.


01:26:50.533 --> 01:26:52.501
Sorry we had to fool you all...


01:26:52.669 --> 01:26:55.502
...but, uh, I promised her
daddy I'd look out for her.


01:26:55.672 --> 01:26:58.937
- It's dangerous for girls to ride the rails.
- Are you mad?


01:26:59.108 --> 01:27:02.009
Of course we're not.
And I like your dress.


01:27:02.745 --> 01:27:05.976
- Thank you.
- Do I have to call you Constance?


01:27:06.282 --> 01:27:07.306
No.


01:27:08.451 --> 01:27:09.748
Good.


01:27:10.086 --> 01:27:13.078
You may be a girl, but you'll
always be a boy to me, Countee.


01:27:13.256 --> 01:27:15.656
Thanks, I think.


01:27:20.063 --> 01:27:21.428
Yeah.


01:27:56.699 --> 01:27:58.098
That looks good.


01:27:58.267 --> 01:28:01.600
How exquisite.
- Lovely, Margaret.


01:28:02.071 --> 01:28:04.699
Well, we certainly have
a lot to be thankful for.


01:28:05.742 --> 01:28:07.869
Hear, hear. To Kit.


01:28:08.044 --> 01:28:11.013
We certainly wouldn't
still be here without you.


01:28:12.482 --> 01:28:14.143
To Kit.


01:28:14.584 --> 01:28:16.176
Your father would be so proud.


01:28:18.254 --> 01:28:21.223
And what do you hear
from Chicago, Margaret?


01:28:29.732 --> 01:28:32.132
Kit, would you please pass
the beans to Miss Dooley?


01:28:32.301 --> 01:28:33.632
Yes.


01:28:48.084 --> 01:28:51.417
Kit. Will? Countee?


01:28:52.155 --> 01:28:53.179
Ma'am.


01:28:54.190 --> 01:28:55.987
Sorry to disturb you all...


01:28:56.159 --> 01:28:59.651
...but this being thanksgiving,
we just wanted to show our thanks.


01:28:59.829 --> 01:29:01.660
It won't be but a minute.


01:29:01.831 --> 01:29:03.458
Come on in.


01:29:03.833 --> 01:29:06.131
- May we?
- Please.


01:29:08.271 --> 01:29:10.603
- Oh, Will.
- We didn't know how else to thank you.


01:29:11.107 --> 01:29:13.371
WO This is yours. Hi. Thank you.


01:29:13.543 --> 01:29:14.840
Hi.


01:29:15.778 --> 01:29:17.336
Hi.
- Hi.


01:29:17.714 --> 01:29:20.182
Can't thank you enough, Miss Kittredge.


01:29:22.485 --> 01:29:25.249
Oh, my good... Will.


01:29:25.421 --> 01:29:27.719
You can make a big stew.
- I've never seen...


01:29:27.890 --> 01:29:29.152
WO Hi. Thank you very much.


01:29:29.325 --> 01:29:31.793
Thank you. Thank you very much.


01:29:37.934 --> 01:29:41.062
Oh, please. Please. Don't go. L...


01:29:41.871 --> 01:29:46.467
I can't think of a better way to
celebrate than with all of you.


01:29:46.642 --> 01:29:49.577
Please, won't you join us?


01:29:49.746 --> 01:29:51.941
We'd... We'd be honored.


01:29:54.684 --> 01:29:56.345
Hello?


01:30:00.156 --> 01:30:01.885
Dad?


01:30:03.960 --> 01:30:05.427
Dad!


01:30:07.930 --> 01:30:09.363
Oh!


01:30:09.532 --> 01:30:10.624
Oh!


01:30:12.001 --> 01:30:16.131
Oh, Kit. Come here. Let me look at you.


01:30:17.607 --> 01:30:19.734
Oh, sweetheart.


01:30:23.780 --> 01:30:27.045
You promised me that you
would write to me once a week.


01:30:27.216 --> 01:30:29.309
- And it's been...
- too long.


01:30:29.485 --> 01:30:30.543
I know, Kit.


01:30:31.254 --> 01:30:34.485
Every day I kept thinking,
"tomorrow there'll be better news. "


01:30:35.558 --> 01:30:37.651
But there never was.


01:30:38.594 --> 01:30:40.221
And then I realized...


01:30:40.596 --> 01:30:42.621
...that if I couldn't face you...


01:30:42.799 --> 01:30:45.859
...or your mother, if I couldn't
tell you what was happening...


01:30:46.469 --> 01:30:48.596
...it was like that old car.


01:30:48.771 --> 01:30:50.295
I was letting it beat me.


01:30:50.940 --> 01:30:52.339
I won't let it beat me, honey.


01:30:55.111 --> 01:30:56.135
Kit?


01:31:01.450 --> 01:31:03.008
Jack?


01:31:11.460 --> 01:31:12.825
I'm here.


01:31:21.003 --> 01:31:22.664
And I'm gonna find a job.


01:31:23.005 --> 01:31:24.996
I promise.


01:31:25.308 --> 01:31:26.570
In Chicago?


01:31:30.279 --> 01:31:32.076
No, Kit.


01:31:32.415 --> 01:31:34.440
Right here in Cincinnati.


01:31:34.617 --> 01:31:36.881
I'm home to stay.


01:31:49.098 --> 01:31:50.861
Want some more juice?


01:31:58.241 --> 01:32:00.539
WO I just thank you. This is delicious.


01:32:00.710 --> 01:32:02.678
Thank you.


01:32:03.246 --> 01:32:04.270
Mr. Pennington.


01:32:05.648 --> 01:32:07.582
Thank you, sir.


01:32:14.223 --> 01:32:17.283
That son you've got around my age...


01:32:18.060 --> 01:32:19.891
...write him, please...


01:32:20.396 --> 01:32:21.556
...tonight...


01:32:22.098 --> 01:32:23.565
...for me.


01:32:30.806 --> 01:32:33.468
Excuse me. Everybody. Wait, wait.


01:32:33.643 --> 01:32:35.508
Quiet, please, everybody.


01:32:37.546 --> 01:32:40.481
Countee has something she has to say.


01:32:40.917 --> 01:32:42.612
Ready?


01:32:48.958 --> 01:32:53.986
"The cow jumped over the moon. "


01:32:54.163 --> 01:32:56.324
What's the cow doing
jumping over the moon?


01:32:56.499 --> 01:32:57.989
Ha, ha! That's just silly.


01:32:58.167 --> 01:33:01.330
All this time I've been trying to
read, and this is what they say?


01:33:03.639 --> 01:33:04.970
Come here. Aw.


01:33:06.375 --> 01:33:08.104
I'm proud of you.


01:33:17.720 --> 01:33:18.778
Hi.


01:33:19.588 --> 01:33:21.419
Kit, sweetheart.


01:33:22.258 --> 01:33:24.385
There's someone here to see you.


01:33:25.227 --> 01:33:27.695
Margaret Mildred Kittredge.


01:33:27.863 --> 01:33:29.353
Mr. Gibson?


01:33:29.799 --> 01:33:33.064
Here you go, kiddo. Hot off the presses.


01:33:37.640 --> 01:33:42.373
- You mean?
- Congratulations, reporter. You're in print!


01:33:42.545 --> 01:33:45.343
Mother, Dad, did you
hear it? I'm in print!


01:33:45.514 --> 01:33:50.247
Oh, sweetheart, I knew you
could do it. I always knew.


01:33:51.020 --> 01:33:52.920
Come on, let's show everybody.


01:33:53.990 --> 01:33:58.484
Mr. Gibson, won't you stay for one drink?
It's a holiday. We're all celebrating.


01:33:58.661 --> 01:34:04.190
- Well, perhaps just one, Mrs...
- Oh, it's miss. Miss Dooley.


01:34:04.367 --> 01:34:06.892
- Huh.
- Hmm.


01:34:07.269 --> 01:34:09.032
But you can call me May.


01:34:09.939 --> 01:34:12.339
That was my mother's name.


01:34:12.508 --> 01:34:14.373
Ha. Golly.


01:34:14.543 --> 01:34:17.103
That is a coincidence.


01:34:20.449 --> 01:34:23.680
Well, what sort of drinks
are you preparing tonight?


01:34:25.254 --> 01:34:29.122
That November, we didn't
dwell on what we'd lost.


01:34:29.291 --> 01:34:32.317
Rather, we celebrated what we had.


01:34:32.495 --> 01:34:33.621
Is everyone ready?


01:34:34.163 --> 01:34:35.187
Yes.


01:34:38.200 --> 01:34:39.963
All right, then.


01:34:47.743 --> 01:34:49.677
We are now one.


01:34:54.350 --> 01:34:57.012
Oh, my gosh.
- I'm one with the monkey.


01:34:57.186 --> 01:34:58.380
Don't tell my mother.


01:35:01.023 --> 01:35:04.550
No, wait. We haven't done
the sacred treehouse pledge.


01:35:05.494 --> 01:35:08.258
The Depression had changed
each one of our lives.


01:35:09.498 --> 01:35:13.662
And while it made us struggle,
it also made us strong.


01:35:13.836 --> 01:35:15.667
Klt & Gwanga, gwanga, galoolie.


01:35:15.838 --> 01:35:18.568
COUNtEE & Gwanga, gwanga, galoolie.


01:35:18.741 --> 01:35:20.834
Kit, look, the monkey's got your camera.


01:35:21.010 --> 01:35:23.570
Now, no matter what life had in store...


01:35:25.247 --> 01:35:27.340
...I was ready.

